1
00:00:00,490 --> 00:00:00,990
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:00,990 --> 00:00:01,970
[CLICKING]

3
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:02,950
[RUSTLING]

4
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:03,920
[CLICKING]

5
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:04,420
[TAPPING]

6
00:00:12,447 --> 00:00:13,280
PHILLIP ISOLA: Good.

7
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,840
So welcome to today's lecture.

8
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:20,560
And today, we're going to talk about representation learning.

9
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:24,020
So we're moving into the next third of the class.

10
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:27,240
So we covered basic approximation and generalization

11
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:29,600
properties of MLPs.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,440
And then we talked about different architectures,

13
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,223
transformers, and RNNs, and so forth.

14
00:00:36,223 --> 00:00:38,640
And now we're going to come to a kind of a new perspective

15
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:42,120
on deep learning, which is the idea of representation learning

16
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:44,640
and generative modeling.

17
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,040
And this is a perspective, which is associated mostly

18
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:52,360
with deep neural networks, but actually could be construed

19
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:53,620
to be broader than this.

20
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,360
You could do representation learning and generative

21
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:56,840
modeling with other architectures

22
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:59,930
that are maybe not neural networks,

23
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:02,305
but most of the techniques have been developed intimately

24
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:03,805
in conjunction with neural networks.

25
00:01:03,805 --> 00:01:05,530
And this field of representation learning

26
00:01:05,530 --> 00:01:08,650
usually goes hand in hand with deep learning.

27
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:13,410
So we'll talk about the idea that deep nets

28
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:14,430
learn representations.

29
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:16,010
What does that even mean?

30
00:01:16,010 --> 00:01:18,770
We'll ask, why should we learn representations?

31
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:22,730
And then we'll look at one kind of representation learner called

32
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:24,610
the autoencoder.

33
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:26,810
We'll look at the relationship to clustering

34
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:29,080
and what's called vector quantization.

35
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,330
And we'll look at the idea of learning representations

36
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:35,730
via prediction and what's called self-supervised learning.

37
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:37,308
And then, in the next lecture, we'll

38
00:01:37,308 --> 00:01:39,850
talk about a different type of representation learning, which

39
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:42,450
is called metric learning or contrastive

40
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:45,850
learning, where you learn about similarities and distances.

41
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:50,370
So this is going to be the new picture for deep learning

42
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:52,650
for the next few weeks.

43
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:55,770
We have data that comes into a system on the bottom.

44
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:57,757
And then layer by layer, we change

45
00:01:57,757 --> 00:01:59,590
the changed the representation of that data,

46
00:01:59,590 --> 00:02:01,132
until we get to an output at the top.

47
00:02:01,132 --> 00:02:04,310
And we'll call the output an embedding or representation.

48
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:07,710
But actually, each layer is a representation of the data.

49
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:11,070
So layer by layer, the deep net takes your raw data

50
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:15,630
x and turns it into f of x, and then f of f of x, and so forth.

51
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:18,377
And each layer is a different representation,

52
00:02:18,377 --> 00:02:20,210
so the data will be differently distributed.

53
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:23,030
It'll be transformed somehow.

54
00:02:23,030 --> 00:02:25,150
And the forward direction through a neural network

55
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:27,650
will be called the encoding direction.

56
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:31,110
So it's going from data to maybe simpler, better, more abstract

57
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:32,655
representations.

58
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:35,030
And that's the direction in which representation learning

59
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:35,530
operates.

60
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:38,470
It tries to find a good mapping from data to embeddings.

61
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:40,890
And the backwards direction is not going to be backprop.

62
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:42,570
Now backprop tries to find the gradient.

63
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:44,570
The backwards direction is more like the inverse

64
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:46,350
of the forward direction in this context.

65
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:47,933
And that's called generative modeling.

66
00:02:47,933 --> 00:02:50,590
It's going from abstract representations and latent

67
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:52,610
variables to data.

68
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:56,400
And we'll talk about generative modeling in a few weeks.

69
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,890
OK.

70
00:02:57,890 --> 00:02:59,640
So these two things can roughly be thought

71
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:00,848
of as inverses of each other.

72
00:03:00,848 --> 00:03:03,538
And we'll clarify that connection as we go.

73
00:03:03,538 --> 00:03:06,080
But today, we're just going to talk about the first direction

74
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:08,120
going forward through a network and view that

75
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:11,480
as learning representations.

76
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:18,680
And one label for this idea is data to vec, so x2vec.

77
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:19,920
X is your data.

78
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,740
And people have developed x2vec for all different modalities.

79
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:27,560
So the most famous early version of x2vec was called word2vec.

80
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:30,640
You want to go from words in English or some other language

81
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:34,008
to representations that are vectors.

82
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:35,300
But you can do this for images.

83
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:36,425
You can do this for sounds.

84
00:03:36,425 --> 00:03:38,920
You can do this for molecules, whatever you want.

85
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:40,417
And here's an example of an image.

86
00:03:40,417 --> 00:03:41,500
We take our data as input.

87
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,100
And then, now, we're thinking, layer by layer,

88
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:45,295
we get a vector representation or maybe a tensor

89
00:03:45,295 --> 00:03:46,420
representation of the data.

90
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,503
And let's investigate what that representation is,

91
00:03:48,503 --> 00:03:52,840
what each of these is indicating a neuron or mathematically just

92
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:55,980
a scalar dimension of a vector.

93
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:58,940
And we can investigate what those actually represent.

94
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:01,980
So we'll talk about the layer 3 representation of this image

95
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,620
is if I go through three layers of neural processing, three

96
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:06,460
convolutional layers, for example,

97
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:08,400
what is the tensor of activations I get out?

98
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,740
That's the representation at layer 3.

99
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:13,660
And then we can talk about other layers, too.

100
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:16,140
So the representations will be a vector or tensor

101
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:17,560
of neural activations.

102
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:19,980
And in this example, they might be representing

103
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:23,740
that there's a car, or there's a ground, or so forth, a building,

104
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:24,970
a road.

105
00:04:24,970 --> 00:04:27,220
So we're going to try to see how representations might

106
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:29,900
map onto these semantic representations

107
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:31,500
that we think about in our head.

108
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:32,340
OK, question?

109
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:36,580
AUDIENCE: With this family of two vectors,

110
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:38,940
are they feature extractions?

111
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:40,980
PHILLIP ISOLA: Feature extraction is the same--

112
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,420
is another name for the same thing, yeah.

113
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:49,500
So x2vec can also be called extracting features

114
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:53,580
from input data x, and the features in the form of vectors

115
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:56,267
or tensors in this case.

116
00:04:56,267 --> 00:04:57,350
We have one more question.

117
00:04:57,350 --> 00:04:58,808
AUDIENCE: Was x2vec, traditionally,

118
00:04:58,808 --> 00:05:01,870
only getting from bigger data sets to smaller representations?

119
00:05:01,870 --> 00:05:03,070
[INAUDIBLE]

120
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:05,205
PHILLIP ISOLA: So the question is, is x2vec always

121
00:05:05,205 --> 00:05:06,830
going to smaller representations, lower

122
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:08,430
dimensional representations?

123
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:11,813
Well, that is one of the classic ways of doing it.

124
00:05:11,813 --> 00:05:13,230
Often, the representation you want

125
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:15,928
to be a smaller object, a lower dimensional

126
00:05:15,928 --> 00:05:17,470
vector than the input, but that's not

127
00:05:17,470 --> 00:05:18,512
the only way of doing it.

128
00:05:18,512 --> 00:05:20,750
You don't have to do it that way.

129
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:22,870
OK, the intuition here is--

130
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:25,370
I'll use these cartoons a few times over the next few weeks,

131
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:27,310
or we'll use them in a few lectures.

132
00:05:27,310 --> 00:05:28,970
So the data will be this blue blob.

133
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:33,430
It's some complicated object, high-dimensional, weird

134
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:34,390
topology.

135
00:05:34,390 --> 00:05:36,850
That's like the space of images.

136
00:05:36,850 --> 00:05:39,830
It's this weird manifold.

137
00:05:39,830 --> 00:05:45,590
And the representation will be this orange or reddish circle.

138
00:05:45,590 --> 00:05:47,830
And it's a circle because we're going

139
00:05:47,830 --> 00:05:50,550
to make the point that the representation is, oftentimes,

140
00:05:50,550 --> 00:05:52,230
a simpler space.

141
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:54,850
So the distribution over embeddings

142
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:56,830
is often going to be a very simple object.

143
00:05:56,830 --> 00:05:59,750
In fact, it will typically be a Gaussian distribution.

144
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:02,770
So Gaussian is a circle in high dimensions,

145
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,490
or Gaussian in high dimensions looks like a circle

146
00:06:05,490 --> 00:06:06,850
OK.

147
00:06:06,850 --> 00:06:09,290
So this is the picture where somehow learning embeddings

148
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:10,070
are simpler.

149
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:12,570
They have a simpler distribution than the data distribution.

150
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:15,270
The input blob is like the data distribution.

151
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:16,770
And we call the mapping from data

152
00:06:16,770 --> 00:06:20,570
to representation an encoder.

153
00:06:20,570 --> 00:06:24,845
So I'm going to now show some visuals.

154
00:06:24,845 --> 00:06:26,970
And I want to introduce a different way of thinking

155
00:06:26,970 --> 00:06:28,930
about functions than what you might have

156
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:31,770
learned about in grade school.

157
00:06:31,770 --> 00:06:36,230
So when you first encountered functions,

158
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:38,150
we learned this way of representing them.

159
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:41,410
You have on the x-axis the input and on the y-axis the output

160
00:06:41,410 --> 00:06:42,552
to your function.

161
00:06:42,552 --> 00:06:44,010
But that's not really the only way.

162
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:47,010
That's basically telling you, what is the mapping from Xin

163
00:06:47,010 --> 00:06:48,990
to Xout, or from x to y, traditionally.

164
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:51,380
But in this class, we usually talk about Xin

165
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:53,900
to Xout for some layer.

166
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,100
So this is just telling me that if I'm

167
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:59,040
at some point on the x-axis, where do I map to on the y-axis?

168
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:01,100
It's telling me something about the mapping.

169
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:05,000
So we're just going to rotate the y-axis to be at the top.

170
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,620
And now we'll look at this mapping

171
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:11,100
as mapping a set of points on the input dimension

172
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:13,060
to the set of points on the output dimension.

173
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,393
And the identity mapping now looks really simple, right?

174
00:07:15,393 --> 00:07:16,440
It's just straight lines.

175
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:17,820
Nothing changed.

176
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:20,017
So I don't know why we decided to go

177
00:07:20,017 --> 00:07:22,100
with this way of representing functions as opposed

178
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:24,007
to this way, but I think both of them

179
00:07:24,007 --> 00:07:26,340
have some interesting properties and advantages in terms

180
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:28,860
of clarifying what's going on.

181
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:32,660
But the second way will help us to visualize

182
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:34,513
some of the mappings in neural networks.

183
00:07:34,513 --> 00:07:36,180
Actually, in some of the earlier slides,

184
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:39,140
we already showed a few examples of this.

185
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:42,300
So here are a few different neural layers for a scalar

186
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:43,480
input to a scalar output.

187
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:46,780
So this is the width of the network is 1, in this case.

188
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:50,990
And what does a linear layer do?

189
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:55,110
A linear layer can-- sorry, if the linear layer has weight 2,

190
00:07:55,110 --> 00:07:56,450
it will just expand.

191
00:07:56,450 --> 00:07:59,910
It'll take your input data, and it will make it scaled up.

192
00:07:59,910 --> 00:08:02,990
So it'll just spread it out.

193
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:04,090
What does a ReLU do?

194
00:08:04,090 --> 00:08:05,670
OK, ReLU is kind of interesting.

195
00:08:05,670 --> 00:08:08,590
A ReLU takes all of your data in the negative half space

196
00:08:08,590 --> 00:08:10,230
and maps it to 0.

197
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:14,230
So this ReLU has this property that the density of data

198
00:08:14,230 --> 00:08:17,110
that will get mapped to 0.

199
00:08:17,110 --> 00:08:18,710
You'll usually have a spike of density

200
00:08:18,710 --> 00:08:21,590
at 0, which we'll see on the next slide.

201
00:08:21,590 --> 00:08:25,370
And the sigmoid will map most of your inputs to either 0 or 1,

202
00:08:25,370 --> 00:08:28,750
but in a soft way.

203
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:31,790
So I like thinking about functions this way

204
00:08:31,790 --> 00:08:34,470
because I think it helps to understand representations

205
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:36,669
and distributional transformations.

206
00:08:36,669 --> 00:08:38,318
So think of your functions as taking

207
00:08:38,318 --> 00:08:40,110
a set, a distribution of input data points,

208
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:43,309
and doing these geometric transformations,

209
00:08:43,309 --> 00:08:47,220
squishing them, and skewing them, and remapping them.

210
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:49,470
And so here are some of the functions that we've seen,

211
00:08:49,470 --> 00:08:51,730
some of the basic neural networks

212
00:08:51,730 --> 00:08:54,050
layers that we've seen in this class.

213
00:08:54,050 --> 00:08:58,170
So we have the linear, the ReLU, the normalization layers,

214
00:08:58,170 --> 00:09:00,130
softmax layers.

215
00:09:00,130 --> 00:09:03,997
And activations are going to be colored in this red font.

216
00:09:03,997 --> 00:09:05,830
And parameters are colored in the blue font,

217
00:09:05,830 --> 00:09:07,832
just to clarify what is actually being learned.

218
00:09:07,832 --> 00:09:10,290
So the only learnable parameters are the weights and biases

219
00:09:10,290 --> 00:09:12,650
in these layers.

220
00:09:12,650 --> 00:09:15,158
So here's the wiring diagrams.

221
00:09:15,158 --> 00:09:17,450
So let's look at the mapping diagrams for these layers,

222
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:20,610
just to build up this intuition.

223
00:09:20,610 --> 00:09:23,250
So we're always mapping from Xin to Xout.

224
00:09:23,250 --> 00:09:28,170
Now one nice property of this other way of plotting functions

225
00:09:28,170 --> 00:09:30,750
is I don't have to only plot scalar to scalar functions.

226
00:09:30,750 --> 00:09:33,430
I can plot 2D to 2D functions.

227
00:09:33,430 --> 00:09:34,680
I don't have to plot 3D to 3D.

228
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:35,847
That's where it breaks down.

229
00:09:35,847 --> 00:09:37,270
But at least I can go to 2D to 2D,

230
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:40,470
which you can't do with traditional plotting.

231
00:09:40,470 --> 00:09:41,890
So here's the mapping.

232
00:09:41,890 --> 00:09:43,690
I'm showing in two different ways.

233
00:09:43,690 --> 00:09:47,580
On the left, I'm showing how each point on a grid of input

234
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:51,140
values maps to the output.

235
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:54,180
And on the right, I'm showing how each point in a Gaussian

236
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:57,560
cloud over the input space maps to the output.

237
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:59,120
So 2D data goes in.

238
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:00,680
And what does the linear layer do?

239
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:02,980
It's going to be an affine transformation.

240
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:05,820
And geometrically, it will just look like rotating, squishing,

241
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:08,075
scaling, anything like this.

242
00:10:08,075 --> 00:10:09,700
So it's like a geometric transformation

243
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:11,660
of the input data.

244
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:13,660
That's all it's doing.

245
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:15,580
What does a ReLU do?

246
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:18,920
So a ReLU looks like this funny shape.

247
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:21,580
So there's two important properties to notice.

248
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:25,900
So one is the ReLU maps all data to the positive orthant,

249
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:26,760
in this case.

250
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:28,343
So in high dimensions, it's just going

251
00:10:28,343 --> 00:10:32,860
to be the subspace where all the dimensions are

252
00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:36,100
positive because anything that's negative gets mapped to 0.

253
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:38,900
And two is you can see how most of the points

254
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,320
get mapped to these axes.

255
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:43,240
You get this what's called a sparse representation.

256
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:45,760
So ReLU has this property of making things sparser

257
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:49,620
because most of the inputs or in each dimension,

258
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:51,483
half of the values will tend to be negative

259
00:10:51,483 --> 00:10:52,900
and half will tend to be positive.

260
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:58,880
And the negative values will get mapped to the axes here.

261
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,253
And there'll be a lot of density right at the origin

262
00:11:02,253 --> 00:11:03,920
because that will correspond to anything

263
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:05,900
in this strictly negative orthant.

264
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:08,680
And in high dimensions, I think this effect becomes even more

265
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:11,800
extreme.

266
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:13,985
So what does L2 norm look like?

267
00:11:13,985 --> 00:11:15,360
And this is what I think I showed

268
00:11:15,360 --> 00:11:18,000
in one of the last lectures, but I didn't really

269
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,960
fully explain it.

270
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:23,680
What L2 norm will look like-- and same with RMSE norm,

271
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:27,240
and same with LayerNorm, which is a variation on this--

272
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:29,020
is it will take all of your data,

273
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:31,260
and it will map it to unitary vectors.

274
00:11:31,260 --> 00:11:33,680
It will map it to vectors that have norm 1.

275
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:35,960
And so in this particular case, with Euclidean norm,

276
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:39,080
it will map it to points on the circle.

277
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:42,280
And high dimensions will map it to points on the hypersphere.

278
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:45,090
So that's what these layers do.

279
00:11:45,090 --> 00:11:49,090
And that's nice for various reasons, but one of them

280
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:53,090
is that we know that the numerics are

281
00:11:53,090 --> 00:11:57,290
going to be bounded somehow.

282
00:11:57,290 --> 00:11:59,870
The vectors will not go to infinity or not go to 0.

283
00:11:59,870 --> 00:12:02,510
They'll all get into a unitary range.

284
00:12:05,885 --> 00:12:08,268
So what is softmax?

285
00:12:08,268 --> 00:12:09,810
Now I'm going to ask you, can anybody

286
00:12:09,810 --> 00:12:11,550
explain, why does softmax look like that?

287
00:12:11,550 --> 00:12:14,958
What is that line that's showing up in the softmax graph?

288
00:12:14,958 --> 00:12:16,750
So I have input data, two-dimensional data,

289
00:12:16,750 --> 00:12:19,050
and I'm doing softmax of those two dimensions.

290
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:21,770
Let's let me see if somebody else-- yeah?

291
00:12:21,770 --> 00:12:25,643
AUDIENCE: x1 plus x2 equals [INAUDIBLE]

292
00:12:25,643 --> 00:12:26,810
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes, exactly.

293
00:12:26,810 --> 00:12:35,237
So the answer was that's a line that x1 plus x2 equals 1,

294
00:12:35,237 --> 00:12:37,570
or x plus y equals 1, if you think of one dimension as y

295
00:12:37,570 --> 00:12:39,330
and one dimension as x.

296
00:12:39,330 --> 00:12:42,428
And that's the simplex.

297
00:12:42,428 --> 00:12:44,470
Is that the one dimensional simplex or the zero--

298
00:12:44,470 --> 00:12:46,262
I think that's the one-dimensional simplex.

299
00:12:46,262 --> 00:12:48,010
I can't remember how to define simplex.

300
00:12:48,010 --> 00:12:53,030
But anyway, the simplex is the set of points in Rd,

301
00:12:53,030 --> 00:12:55,270
where the dimensions sum to 1.

302
00:12:55,270 --> 00:12:57,870
And the output of a softmax is going

303
00:12:57,870 --> 00:13:00,110
to be a point in the simplex.

304
00:13:00,110 --> 00:13:01,850
So that's what that looks like.

305
00:13:01,850 --> 00:13:05,870
This is, basically, going to be your probability is

306
00:13:05,870 --> 00:13:08,110
high of one class, if you're at that point,

307
00:13:08,110 --> 00:13:12,550
and low for that class if you're at the other point.

308
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:15,930
So this is what neural networks are doing.

309
00:13:15,930 --> 00:13:18,128
Those are our basic layers.

310
00:13:18,128 --> 00:13:20,670
It's a little more complicated with attention and other kinds

311
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:22,790
of layers, but these are the basic ones.

312
00:13:22,790 --> 00:13:28,230
So let's now look at what an MLP looks like plotted this way.

313
00:13:28,230 --> 00:13:28,730
Let's see.

314
00:13:28,730 --> 00:13:30,438
This is a three-layer MLP because there's

315
00:13:30,438 --> 00:13:33,110
three linear layers, so our convention is three layers.

316
00:13:33,110 --> 00:13:35,750
And the input data is going to be

317
00:13:35,750 --> 00:13:38,630
a cloud of points at the origin with label red

318
00:13:38,630 --> 00:13:42,880
and a hemisphere of points around with label blue.

319
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:46,440
So we're going to try to do a binary classification.

320
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:48,680
So this will be training with cross-entropy loss

321
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:51,040
doing softmax regression.

322
00:13:51,040 --> 00:13:53,603
And we're going to look at what training actually looks like.

323
00:13:53,603 --> 00:13:55,520
So this is a non-linear classification problem

324
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:57,280
because there's no linear hyperplane that

325
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:00,760
separates the red cloud from the blue cloud.

326
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:03,440
So layer by layer, the representation

327
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:06,022
of the distribution of data gets transformed.

328
00:14:06,022 --> 00:14:07,480
And what you're going to see happen

329
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:10,300
is that the red points will move away from the blue points.

330
00:14:10,300 --> 00:14:12,100
And this is what it looks like.

331
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:15,080
So after a few steps of gradient descent,

332
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:16,960
they start to spread out like this.

333
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:22,160
And at this layer we're shifting things over.

334
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,340
And then the ReLU snaps it back onto the axes.

335
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:26,860
And then we skew things in a different direction.

336
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:29,580
And eventually, we get things to spread out on a line.

337
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:33,760
And then the softmax clamps that back down to this simplex.

338
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:37,213
And so the red points are all heading toward the 0.10.

339
00:14:37,213 --> 00:14:39,380
And the blue points are all heading toward the 0.01.

340
00:14:39,380 --> 00:14:41,580
And those are the one-hot labels for your data.

341
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:43,342
So this optimization is actually working

342
00:14:43,342 --> 00:14:45,300
at segregating the blue points to be classified

343
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:49,340
as class II and the red points to be classified as class 1.

344
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:51,390
So let's look at that animated.

345
00:14:51,390 --> 00:14:52,640
So this is what it looks like.

346
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:56,820
This is every single layer has width 2.

347
00:14:56,820 --> 00:14:58,202
So we can plot everything here.

348
00:14:58,202 --> 00:14:59,160
There's nothing hidden.

349
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:00,980
It's not like I'm doing dimensionality reduction

350
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:01,760
or visualizing.

351
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:06,620
It's just the raw activation values at each of these layers.

352
00:15:06,620 --> 00:15:10,460
And each of the layers now can be understood

353
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:13,020
as a different representation of the data distribution

354
00:15:13,020 --> 00:15:15,620
and a better and better representation

355
00:15:15,620 --> 00:15:17,300
for solving the class of segregating

356
00:15:17,300 --> 00:15:19,050
the red points from the blue points, which

357
00:15:19,050 --> 00:15:21,500
is the classification task.

358
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:23,780
OK.

359
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:30,370
Oh, yeah, just for fun, I didn't really have a deep insight

360
00:15:30,370 --> 00:15:31,120
to take from this.

361
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:34,780
But just for fun, you can compare the optimization

362
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:38,992
using SGD, gradient descent, to the optimization using

363
00:15:38,992 --> 00:15:40,950
the spectral descent method that you worked out

364
00:15:40,950 --> 00:15:42,670
in your problem set 2.

365
00:15:42,670 --> 00:15:45,190
So in the spectral descent method,

366
00:15:45,190 --> 00:15:48,870
you are normalizing the updates by their spectral norm.

367
00:15:48,870 --> 00:15:51,967
And you can see there's some interesting different dynamics.

368
00:15:51,967 --> 00:15:54,050
It's a little bit hard to read too much into this.

369
00:15:54,050 --> 00:15:56,150
Well, the spectral descent is descending faster,

370
00:15:56,150 --> 00:15:58,775
but that depends on the learning rate, so there's some caveats.

371
00:15:58,775 --> 00:16:00,210
But it is getting to a lower loss.

372
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:01,918
The loss is tiny, little font up here,

373
00:16:01,918 --> 00:16:04,210
is actually segregating the red and blue points better.

374
00:16:04,210 --> 00:16:07,150
So you can understand that different optimization schemes

375
00:16:07,150 --> 00:16:09,048
actually do perform differently.

376
00:16:09,048 --> 00:16:11,590
And there was some reason for working out what you worked out

377
00:16:11,590 --> 00:16:12,550
in your problem set.

378
00:16:12,550 --> 00:16:14,550
I think something that might be interesting here

379
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:17,750
is it looks like this first linear layer is

380
00:16:17,750 --> 00:16:21,730
almost like a rotation, which is a orthogonal transformation.

381
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:24,510
And if your updates are orthogonalized,

382
00:16:24,510 --> 00:16:28,638
then maybe that somehow relates to this first layer finding

383
00:16:28,638 --> 00:16:29,930
this orthogonal transformation.

384
00:16:29,930 --> 00:16:33,220
It's just a rotation, but it's also scaling.

385
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:35,470
I think it might be fun to see if you can get insights

386
00:16:35,470 --> 00:16:38,060
from this, but it's a little complicated.

387
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:45,080
So we can also try visualizing more advanced nets in this way.

388
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:48,080
And this is where the power of the ideas of representation

389
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:49,120
learning really come in.

390
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:52,640
Not when I'm just having an MLP over two-dimensional embeddings,

391
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:58,720
but when I am having a very high-dimensional feature space.

392
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:00,620
So this is the CLIP neural network.

393
00:17:00,620 --> 00:17:04,119
It's a computer vision system that

394
00:17:04,119 --> 00:17:07,960
is quite good at extracting good features from images.

395
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:11,079
We'll talk about that a little bit later in the course.

396
00:17:11,079 --> 00:17:14,440
And now the embeddings are not two-dimensional,

397
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:18,339
but I am doing a dimensionality reduction technique, PCA,

398
00:17:18,339 --> 00:17:19,948
to show what they look like.

399
00:17:19,948 --> 00:17:22,240
So it's a bit of a cartoon rather than just showing you

400
00:17:22,240 --> 00:17:22,859
the real data.

401
00:17:22,859 --> 00:17:24,859
But the point is that layer by layer-- and these

402
00:17:24,859 --> 00:17:27,240
are vision transformer layers or a block

403
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:30,880
of three-vision transformer blocks.

404
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:33,440
In the transforming, you have attention, and then

405
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:36,560
LayerNorm, and then softmax, and then this and that.

406
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:40,222
So this is an entire block of sequence of layers.

407
00:17:40,222 --> 00:17:42,180
But what happens is as you go deeper and deeper

408
00:17:42,180 --> 00:17:44,842
into the network, you end up segregating

409
00:17:44,842 --> 00:17:45,800
these different colors.

410
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:49,100
And the colors are corresponding to the class of images

411
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,600
in a computer vision data set.

412
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:51,360
So is it a cat?

413
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:52,260
Is it a dog?

414
00:17:52,260 --> 00:17:53,600
And you see that at the top of the network,

415
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:55,000
they're all very separated from each other.

416
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,880
And that's because this network was trained for classification.

417
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:01,020
So it's trained to find a way to disentangle

418
00:18:01,020 --> 00:18:03,300
all of these different classes and have

419
00:18:03,300 --> 00:18:05,620
their representation in some high-dimensional space

420
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:07,960
get all separated so that then, at the very end,

421
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:10,420
I can just take a softmax of that.

422
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:14,700
And they'll be cleanly separated to the vertices

423
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:17,140
of the simplex, which is the one-hot labels

424
00:18:17,140 --> 00:18:18,780
for these classes.

425
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:22,552
So it's a little bit hard to see because, again, it's doing PCA,

426
00:18:22,552 --> 00:18:24,260
so it's not actually showing the simplex.

427
00:18:24,260 --> 00:18:27,380
But it's something kind of like that.

428
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:30,260
So the whole point is just to build up

429
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:33,910
the intuition of how we go from entangled, complicated data

430
00:18:33,910 --> 00:18:36,570
at the input to a neural network to, layer by layer,

431
00:18:36,570 --> 00:18:38,110
gradually morphing, disentangling

432
00:18:38,110 --> 00:18:40,030
it through these geometric transformations,

433
00:18:40,030 --> 00:18:44,030
until you get clean separation of your semantics of interest

434
00:18:44,030 --> 00:18:46,030
that can be read off into a prediction,

435
00:18:46,030 --> 00:18:47,643
for example, classification.

436
00:18:47,643 --> 00:18:49,310
And the output space is a simpler object

437
00:18:49,310 --> 00:18:50,250
than the input space.

438
00:18:50,250 --> 00:18:53,630
That's the point I'm trying to make.

439
00:18:53,630 --> 00:18:55,550
OK.

440
00:18:55,550 --> 00:18:56,050
Cool.

441
00:18:56,050 --> 00:18:58,825
So any questions about the general idea of representation

442
00:18:58,825 --> 00:19:00,950
learning, and what these neural networks are doing,

443
00:19:00,950 --> 00:19:02,730
and what these visualizations represent?

444
00:19:05,330 --> 00:19:05,830
OK.

445
00:19:05,830 --> 00:19:06,710
Cool.

446
00:19:06,710 --> 00:19:12,570
So one of the initial inspirations for deep learning,

447
00:19:12,570 --> 00:19:15,350
of course, was to understand the human brain.

448
00:19:15,350 --> 00:19:21,270
And in neuroscience, people have studied how representations

449
00:19:21,270 --> 00:19:23,630
are learned in the brain.

450
00:19:23,630 --> 00:19:27,350
So I wanted to make this connection.

451
00:19:27,350 --> 00:19:30,010
So this is a map of the visual cortex,

452
00:19:30,010 --> 00:19:33,530
a cartoon of the visual cortex of the human brain.

453
00:19:33,530 --> 00:19:35,470
And it goes through a sequence of layers.

454
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:37,970
The layers are just like layers in our neural networks.

455
00:19:37,970 --> 00:19:41,310
And they are linear followed by pointwise nonlinear operations,

456
00:19:41,310 --> 00:19:44,010
plus some extra complexities that biologists debate over

457
00:19:44,010 --> 00:19:46,490
whether or not these extra complexities are important

458
00:19:46,490 --> 00:19:46,990
or not.

459
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:51,050
But for us, it's just linear ReLU, linear ReLU.

460
00:19:51,050 --> 00:19:54,050
And in visual cortex, there's about six of these layers

461
00:19:54,050 --> 00:19:55,422
or five or six of these layers.

462
00:19:55,422 --> 00:19:57,130
And then there's a final layer called IT,

463
00:19:57,130 --> 00:20:00,350
where that's thought to be where the semantics are segregated,

464
00:20:00,350 --> 00:20:03,250
where object recognition has occurred.

465
00:20:03,250 --> 00:20:06,570
And so we have first layers extract,

466
00:20:06,570 --> 00:20:07,902
do filtering to find edges.

467
00:20:07,902 --> 00:20:09,610
And the next layers look for conjunctions

468
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:13,170
of edges, and disjunctions, and all kinds of combinations

469
00:20:13,170 --> 00:20:15,050
until you get pattern recognizers.

470
00:20:15,050 --> 00:20:17,570
And if you look at the neurons, and you poke into the brain,

471
00:20:17,570 --> 00:20:18,930
and you measure what they're responsive to,

472
00:20:18,930 --> 00:20:21,230
you'll see that as you go deeper into the brain,

473
00:20:21,230 --> 00:20:23,450
they become responsive selectively for more

474
00:20:23,450 --> 00:20:25,650
and more complex patterns.

475
00:20:25,650 --> 00:20:28,290
So let's see, do deep nets artificial deep nets do

476
00:20:28,290 --> 00:20:30,850
the same thing?

477
00:20:30,850 --> 00:20:33,060
What do they learn internally?

478
00:20:33,060 --> 00:20:34,820
OK, so let's say I train a deep net

479
00:20:34,820 --> 00:20:36,820
to do classification of images.

480
00:20:36,820 --> 00:20:38,652
Now I'm going to try to probe and interpret

481
00:20:38,652 --> 00:20:40,860
the representation that's learned in order to perform

482
00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:42,520
that classification task.

483
00:20:42,520 --> 00:20:44,980
And we'll do it just like the neuroscientists do.

484
00:20:44,980 --> 00:20:49,253
So we will stick artificial probes into the neurons,

485
00:20:49,253 --> 00:20:51,420
and measure their value as a function of the inputs,

486
00:20:51,420 --> 00:20:55,300
and see what inputs these neurons are responsive to.

487
00:20:55,300 --> 00:20:58,500
So in the brain, we would stick an electrode in

488
00:20:58,500 --> 00:20:59,880
and see when the neuron fires.

489
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:01,560
That's when the ReLU is above 0.

490
00:21:01,560 --> 00:21:03,520
But it might not be exactly ReLU in the brain.

491
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:06,260
It might be some other threshold function.

492
00:21:06,260 --> 00:21:08,180
And we're going to do the same thing.

493
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:10,060
So for which inputs does the neuron turn on?

494
00:21:10,060 --> 00:21:13,660
And for which inputs does it turn off?

495
00:21:13,660 --> 00:21:17,585
So people have been doing this for a while.

496
00:21:17,585 --> 00:21:19,460
One of the modern names for this type of work

497
00:21:19,460 --> 00:21:22,180
is interpretability or mechanistic interpretability

498
00:21:22,180 --> 00:21:23,140
sometimes.

499
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,540
So it's a really interesting area

500
00:21:24,540 --> 00:21:28,200
if you've come across those terms, but it's an old problem.

501
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:32,840
And I'll show you some results from this Zeiler and Fergus 2014

502
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:36,320
paper on trying to interpret the units within a CNN.

503
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:37,360
OK.

504
00:21:37,360 --> 00:21:43,260
So the first layer in the CNN that they were working with,

505
00:21:43,260 --> 00:21:47,000
this was just a vanilla convolutional network

506
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,040
with five or six layers.

507
00:21:50,040 --> 00:21:53,200
Well, the first layer has a set of filters.

508
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:56,420
So the filters will all output a feature map.

509
00:21:56,420 --> 00:21:59,360
So each filter will output some spatially varying

510
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:00,680
set of activations.

511
00:22:00,680 --> 00:22:05,960
And we can look at what input patches in the image

512
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:11,380
are most strongly activating one of the filters to fire.

513
00:22:11,380 --> 00:22:14,000
So what is the filter looking for?

514
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:20,720
So here are nine filters in this montage of nine subgrids.

515
00:22:20,720 --> 00:22:23,440
And on each of these sub areas, we

516
00:22:23,440 --> 00:22:28,490
have the top nine input patches that most activated

517
00:22:28,490 --> 00:22:29,510
that particular filter.

518
00:22:29,510 --> 00:22:33,255
So this is channel 1 filter.

519
00:22:33,255 --> 00:22:34,630
And what do you think it's doing?

520
00:22:34,630 --> 00:22:39,906
So what is this a filter for, this top left one?

521
00:22:39,906 --> 00:22:41,330
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

522
00:22:41,330 --> 00:22:42,510
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, it's like an edge detector.

523
00:22:42,510 --> 00:22:44,050
It's looking for an oriented line,

524
00:22:44,050 --> 00:22:47,302
a bright followed by a dark transition, so, right, filter.

525
00:22:47,302 --> 00:22:48,510
What is the idea of a filter?

526
00:22:48,510 --> 00:22:52,250
A filter is trying to filter out something from the data,

527
00:22:52,250 --> 00:22:55,070
find the thing it's looking for and throw away everything else.

528
00:22:55,070 --> 00:22:58,547
That's what the word filter means, the English word filter.

529
00:22:58,547 --> 00:22:59,630
So that's what it's doing.

530
00:22:59,630 --> 00:23:01,330
It's just it's responding to some things

531
00:23:01,330 --> 00:23:02,330
and not to other things.

532
00:23:02,330 --> 00:23:04,530
And what it responds to is edges.

533
00:23:04,530 --> 00:23:05,170
OK.

534
00:23:05,170 --> 00:23:07,650
And then we have other-- this is a different orientation

535
00:23:07,650 --> 00:23:08,230
of the edge.

536
00:23:08,230 --> 00:23:10,430
And this is like a greenness detector.

537
00:23:10,430 --> 00:23:12,850
It fires selectively when it sees green.

538
00:23:12,850 --> 00:23:13,350
OK.

539
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:14,808
So it looks like at the first layer

540
00:23:14,808 --> 00:23:17,170
of processing in a deep neural net,

541
00:23:17,170 --> 00:23:21,870
you get these simple feature detectors

542
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:24,630
So the next layer, what is that going to look like?

543
00:23:24,630 --> 00:23:26,670
So it turns out not so different than what

544
00:23:26,670 --> 00:23:28,420
the neuroscientists came up with when they

545
00:23:28,420 --> 00:23:30,100
were studying animal brains.

546
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:33,020
So the second layer looks like it's

547
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:35,340
having neurons filters that are selective

548
00:23:35,340 --> 00:23:37,580
not for edges, but for some conjunction of edges,

549
00:23:37,580 --> 00:23:38,960
higher-order patterns.

550
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:42,060
So if I have two channels that detect

551
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:43,940
two different orientations of edges,

552
00:23:43,940 --> 00:23:45,860
the next layer of my network could

553
00:23:45,860 --> 00:23:49,300
look for a conjunction of those two orientations, like a cross.

554
00:23:49,300 --> 00:23:53,300
And that turns out to map to maybe even more complicated

555
00:23:53,300 --> 00:23:56,460
things, like gradients, or circles, and so forth.

556
00:23:56,460 --> 00:23:58,180
So then as we go layer by layer, we're

557
00:23:58,180 --> 00:24:00,780
going to get more and more complex pattern detectors.

558
00:24:00,780 --> 00:24:03,860
So here are the image patches that most strongly

559
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:07,820
activate nine different filters on layer 3 of this network.

560
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:10,240
And already you can start to see, just layer 3,

561
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:13,740
we get selectivity for faces right.

562
00:24:13,740 --> 00:24:17,800
And it's not because it's really recognizing humans necessarily,

563
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:20,380
but maybe it's because it's recognizing

564
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:23,100
little circle of darkness around a bright background

565
00:24:23,100 --> 00:24:26,580
with a broader torso thing underneath it,

566
00:24:26,580 --> 00:24:28,600
so some blobbiness detector.

567
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:32,200
But it happens to be an OK template for a human.

568
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:35,000
And then as we go deeper, we get much more selectivity.

569
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,280
And eventually, we start actually detecting,

570
00:24:38,280 --> 00:24:41,100
selectively segregating dog faces from human faces,

571
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:42,160
for example.

572
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:43,677
And again, this is 2014.

573
00:24:43,677 --> 00:24:45,760
This is the fifth layer of a convolutional network

574
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:47,120
from that era.

575
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:49,320
If you run the same types of methods

576
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:53,160
on modern networks, which are dozens or hundreds of layers,

577
00:24:53,160 --> 00:24:57,088
you will get very precise detectors

578
00:24:57,088 --> 00:24:59,380
deep in the network that code for very specific things.

579
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:01,640
And that's this area of mechanistic interpretability,

580
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:03,440
where people are trying to understand,

581
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:06,400
what are the emergent detectors in these systems?

582
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:09,400
And you can do it for images, or language models, or other things

583
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:09,940
as well.

584
00:25:09,940 --> 00:25:12,800
We can say, is there a neuron within a language model

585
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:15,350
that codes for the sentiment?

586
00:25:15,350 --> 00:25:17,600
There was a famous paper called "The Sentiment Neuron"

587
00:25:17,600 --> 00:25:19,475
that found a neuron that codes for sentiment.

588
00:25:19,475 --> 00:25:21,680
But now this is well known, that this just occurs

589
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:24,410
in all of our modern deep nets.

590
00:25:24,410 --> 00:25:26,570
OK.

591
00:25:26,570 --> 00:25:32,290
So the rough story is that deep nets and the neuroscience models

592
00:25:32,290 --> 00:25:35,810
of the brain are quite in alignment here.

593
00:25:35,810 --> 00:25:39,210
The first layer of the human visual system or monkey

594
00:25:39,210 --> 00:25:41,370
visual system, to be more precise,

595
00:25:41,370 --> 00:25:43,210
will have these edge detectors.

596
00:25:43,210 --> 00:25:46,190
And the next layer will have shape and pattern detectors.

597
00:25:46,190 --> 00:25:48,650
And then, finally, at IT at the sixth layer or so,

598
00:25:48,650 --> 00:25:53,373
we'll have dog face recognition.

599
00:25:53,373 --> 00:25:55,290
One difference-- so there are some differences

600
00:25:55,290 --> 00:25:57,530
between brains and machines.

601
00:25:57,530 --> 00:25:59,930
One interesting difference is that the brain seems

602
00:25:59,930 --> 00:26:02,423
to only have about seven--

603
00:26:02,423 --> 00:26:04,090
it depends how you count, but maybe like

604
00:26:04,090 --> 00:26:06,930
seven layers of convolutional filters.

605
00:26:06,930 --> 00:26:08,770
And our modern architectures have

606
00:26:08,770 --> 00:26:14,250
hundreds of layers of filters and other operations.

607
00:26:14,250 --> 00:26:17,210
But the brain has recurrence, so it uses those seven layers over

608
00:26:17,210 --> 00:26:18,030
and over again.

609
00:26:21,390 --> 00:26:25,070
So let's now get a little bit more formal

610
00:26:25,070 --> 00:26:26,910
and define what a representation is

611
00:26:26,910 --> 00:26:29,910
for the context of this class.

612
00:26:29,910 --> 00:26:31,950
So we're going to, basically, restrict ourselves

613
00:26:31,950 --> 00:26:35,870
to representation as a vector embedding, is a vector.

614
00:26:35,870 --> 00:26:38,013
Or it could be a more general tensor object.

615
00:26:38,013 --> 00:26:39,430
It could have multiple dimensions,

616
00:26:39,430 --> 00:26:43,210
but most canonically, it will be a vector.

617
00:26:43,210 --> 00:26:45,190
And there's two different ways we're going

618
00:26:45,190 --> 00:26:47,852
to use the word representation.

619
00:26:47,852 --> 00:26:50,310
So the first way is we'll say a representation of some data

620
00:26:50,310 --> 00:26:54,110
domain x or maybe a data distribution p of x, probability

621
00:26:54,110 --> 00:26:57,870
of x, is a function that maps from data in that domain

622
00:26:57,870 --> 00:27:01,750
to embeddings in Rd, vector embeddings.

623
00:27:01,750 --> 00:27:04,532
That function f is called the encoder.

624
00:27:04,532 --> 00:27:05,990
And it will assign a feature vector

625
00:27:05,990 --> 00:27:08,110
to every element in the input domain.

626
00:27:08,110 --> 00:27:10,310
And we'll say that function f, that's

627
00:27:10,310 --> 00:27:13,090
the representation of that data domain.

628
00:27:13,090 --> 00:27:16,050
So what is actually parameterizing f?

629
00:27:16,050 --> 00:27:17,467
It's the weights and biases.

630
00:27:17,467 --> 00:27:19,550
So if I have a neural network, the representation,

631
00:27:19,550 --> 00:27:22,880
by this definition, is its weights and biases.

632
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:26,057
That's a learned representation of the data distribution.

633
00:27:26,057 --> 00:27:27,640
Another use of the word representation

634
00:27:27,640 --> 00:27:29,680
will be the representation not of the domain

635
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:31,900
or the distribution, but of a single point.

636
00:27:31,900 --> 00:27:34,480
And that will just be a vector z in Rd,

637
00:27:34,480 --> 00:27:37,460
where z is equal to the encoder applied to f.

638
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:40,428
So people use both of these definitions of representation.

639
00:27:40,428 --> 00:27:42,720
If you want to be clear, you can say the representation

640
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:46,090
of a data point versus the representation of a data domain.

641
00:27:46,090 --> 00:27:47,840
But I wanted to point out that distinction

642
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:50,568
because we'll talk about the learned representation.

643
00:27:50,568 --> 00:27:52,360
And when we say the learned representation,

644
00:27:52,360 --> 00:27:54,740
we usually mean f, OK?

645
00:27:54,740 --> 00:27:56,240
So that's the learned representation

646
00:27:56,240 --> 00:27:59,680
is the weights and biases that fit the data.

647
00:28:03,240 --> 00:28:04,680
So that's the formalism we'll work

648
00:28:04,680 --> 00:28:06,420
with for the next few lectures.

649
00:28:06,420 --> 00:28:10,800
So why learn representations?

650
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:14,680
So there's a lot of answers you could come up with.

651
00:28:14,680 --> 00:28:18,940
But maybe the simplest is to do more learning, right?

652
00:28:18,940 --> 00:28:20,980
So we learn to learn.

653
00:28:20,980 --> 00:28:22,140
It's interesting.

654
00:28:22,140 --> 00:28:26,100
It seems a little bit circular, but this is usually

655
00:28:26,100 --> 00:28:29,240
the context in which we're going to use representations.

656
00:28:29,240 --> 00:28:32,372
We're going to use them to accelerate future learning.

657
00:28:32,372 --> 00:28:34,580
We'll have a few lectures on transfer learning, where

658
00:28:34,580 --> 00:28:35,560
we get into more details.

659
00:28:35,560 --> 00:28:37,393
But I have to introduce a little bit of this

660
00:28:37,393 --> 00:28:41,260
now because the main use of trained representations

661
00:28:41,260 --> 00:28:43,900
is to accelerate future learning.

662
00:28:43,900 --> 00:28:47,020
So quote in Goodfellow, "Generally speaking, a good"--

663
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:49,780
or the Deep Learning book-- "a good representation is one that

664
00:28:49,780 --> 00:28:52,900
makes subsequent learning easier."

665
00:28:52,900 --> 00:28:59,612
And I think this was a strange misconception in the early days

666
00:28:59,612 --> 00:29:00,320
of deep learning.

667
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:05,140
It was that we will make deep networks that

668
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:06,440
act as blank slates.

669
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:11,380
They come at new problems with no pretrained prior information.

670
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:15,820
And people would complain about deep learning as being very data

671
00:29:15,820 --> 00:29:16,540
hungry.

672
00:29:16,540 --> 00:29:20,310
It would take massive data to apply deep net to your problem.

673
00:29:20,310 --> 00:29:23,970
And they would say, well, humans are much more sample efficient.

674
00:29:23,970 --> 00:29:28,030
We only take a little bit of data to solve a new problem

675
00:29:28,030 --> 00:29:31,510
But it wasn't apples to apples.

676
00:29:31,510 --> 00:29:33,470
So humans are born.

677
00:29:33,470 --> 00:29:37,470
And you in this class are coming to learn new information not as

678
00:29:37,470 --> 00:29:38,090
a blank slate.

679
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:41,070
You've gone through billions of years of evolution,

680
00:29:41,070 --> 00:29:43,430
tens of billions of years of the physical evolution

681
00:29:43,430 --> 00:29:45,270
of the universe before that, before even

682
00:29:45,270 --> 00:29:46,790
biological evolution.

683
00:29:46,790 --> 00:29:50,950
And you're also having 20 plus years of pre-training

684
00:29:50,950 --> 00:29:51,730
in your lifetime.

685
00:29:51,730 --> 00:29:53,670
You've learned very good internal skills,

686
00:29:53,670 --> 00:29:55,670
and knowledge, and representations, so you're

687
00:29:55,670 --> 00:29:56,870
not blank slates.

688
00:29:56,870 --> 00:29:58,990
And deep nets also should generally not

689
00:29:58,990 --> 00:30:00,920
be used as blank slates.

690
00:30:00,920 --> 00:30:03,170
This is something that's changed over the last decade.

691
00:30:03,170 --> 00:30:06,190
It used to be we thought deep nets require a lot of data

692
00:30:06,190 --> 00:30:07,690
because we'll start from scratch.

693
00:30:07,690 --> 00:30:10,190
We'll initialize them randomly, and they'll be blank slates.

694
00:30:10,190 --> 00:30:12,310
But these days, this is not really what we do.

695
00:30:12,310 --> 00:30:14,590
We start with pretrained representations.

696
00:30:14,590 --> 00:30:20,840
So let's look at that idea.

697
00:30:20,840 --> 00:30:24,170
So very often, we will have-- let's imagine

698
00:30:24,170 --> 00:30:25,920
that you're working for some music company

699
00:30:25,920 --> 00:30:27,680
at Spotify or something.

700
00:30:27,680 --> 00:30:29,960
And you will train your neural network

701
00:30:29,960 --> 00:30:33,240
to classify the genre of the music.

702
00:30:33,240 --> 00:30:38,080
So in two parts of your network, you'll have some encoder f,

703
00:30:38,080 --> 00:30:42,040
and then you might have some extra readout operation

704
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:44,760
at the end to do your classification.

705
00:30:44,760 --> 00:30:47,000
We'll think of w as just being like a linear layer

706
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:48,800
on top of a representation z.

707
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:51,520
But we can actually select any layer in the network

708
00:30:51,520 --> 00:30:53,800
to be called the representation, any prior layers

709
00:30:53,800 --> 00:30:55,720
are the encoder, and any subsequent layers

710
00:30:55,720 --> 00:30:59,540
are like the readout, OK?

711
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:01,720
But often, what we're going to be tested on

712
00:31:01,720 --> 00:31:04,540
is not going to be the same as what we've trained on.

713
00:31:04,540 --> 00:31:10,760
Maybe we invested $1 billion to train the Spotify sound

714
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:12,620
recognition system.

715
00:31:12,620 --> 00:31:16,740
We don't want to use $1 billion to solve

716
00:31:16,740 --> 00:31:18,780
a new task because maybe the next year,

717
00:31:18,780 --> 00:31:20,300
we realize, actually, we also need

718
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:22,880
to predict if users the music or don't like the music,

719
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:25,380
so we can give them recommendations.

720
00:31:25,380 --> 00:31:30,660
So the trick is that we will just use our same encoder up

721
00:31:30,660 --> 00:31:34,420
to some layer in the network, and we will only

722
00:31:34,420 --> 00:31:36,763
retrain the final readout.

723
00:31:36,763 --> 00:31:39,180
And that might, in this case, be a linear operation, which

724
00:31:39,180 --> 00:31:41,580
would be called a linear probe, or it could

725
00:31:41,580 --> 00:31:44,420
be MLP, or something else.

726
00:31:44,420 --> 00:31:48,760
So we're not going to retrain from scratch.

727
00:31:48,760 --> 00:31:50,820
We're going to initialize our network

728
00:31:50,820 --> 00:31:52,397
with the pre-trained representation.

729
00:31:52,397 --> 00:31:53,980
And in this case, we might just freeze

730
00:31:53,980 --> 00:31:56,188
that pre-trained representation, no longer update it,

731
00:31:56,188 --> 00:32:00,420
and just learn a linear mapping onto our new prediction problem.

732
00:32:00,420 --> 00:32:03,440
So these are the two phases.

733
00:32:03,440 --> 00:32:06,340
Now they have new names for these, pre-training

734
00:32:06,340 --> 00:32:07,620
and post-training.

735
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:08,890
But we used to call it--

736
00:32:08,890 --> 00:32:10,640
there's a lot of different names for this,

737
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:13,260
but you pre-train and then you post-train.

738
00:32:13,260 --> 00:32:17,270
So another thing you can do is not just

739
00:32:17,270 --> 00:32:19,590
train this read-off head.

740
00:32:19,590 --> 00:32:21,790
You can actually update the parameters

741
00:32:21,790 --> 00:32:26,330
of your encoder, as well, but initialize from the prior f.

742
00:32:26,330 --> 00:32:29,390
So you just continue to do backprop

743
00:32:29,390 --> 00:32:33,470
to update to find some fine-tuned perturbation

744
00:32:33,470 --> 00:32:36,390
of your parameters that will solve the new problem.

745
00:32:36,390 --> 00:32:37,730
So this is called fine-tuning.

746
00:32:37,730 --> 00:32:41,150
I'll talk about that on the next slide as well.

747
00:32:41,150 --> 00:32:44,810
So we initialize F prime not with random weights,

748
00:32:44,810 --> 00:32:46,810
but we initialize it with the same weights as f,

749
00:32:46,810 --> 00:32:47,893
and then we keep training.

750
00:32:47,893 --> 00:32:51,150
That's the whole idea of fine-tuning.

751
00:32:51,150 --> 00:32:53,490
And here's the transfer learning paradigm.

752
00:32:53,490 --> 00:32:57,135
This is what is typically done for real-world problems.

753
00:32:57,135 --> 00:32:59,010
For toy problems, you can train from scratch.

754
00:32:59,010 --> 00:33:01,230
But for real problems, you generally can't.

755
00:33:01,230 --> 00:33:03,590
So you have three phases.

756
00:33:03,590 --> 00:33:06,170
You have now not just train and test.

757
00:33:06,170 --> 00:33:09,710
You have pre-train, which can be thought of as learning

758
00:33:09,710 --> 00:33:10,490
a representation.

759
00:33:10,490 --> 00:33:13,070
You do that on a lot of data.

760
00:33:13,070 --> 00:33:15,070
And typically, you can do that on a lot of data.

761
00:33:15,070 --> 00:33:16,820
You can afford to do that on a lot of data

762
00:33:16,820 --> 00:33:19,623
because the pre-training phase, the representation learning

763
00:33:19,623 --> 00:33:21,290
phase, doesn't require as much knowledge

764
00:33:21,290 --> 00:33:22,790
about what the final task is going to be.

765
00:33:22,790 --> 00:33:25,050
We'll see that autoencoders and contrastive learning

766
00:33:25,050 --> 00:33:27,650
are these pre-training methods that don't require knowing what

767
00:33:27,650 --> 00:33:29,370
the final task is going to be.

768
00:33:29,370 --> 00:33:33,090
But then you adapt to your actual task of interest.

769
00:33:33,090 --> 00:33:35,360
And you can do that on just a little data

770
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:37,610
because you already have learned a good representation

771
00:33:37,610 --> 00:33:40,490
of the domain f, of the data domain x

772
00:33:40,490 --> 00:33:41,490
through this function f.

773
00:33:41,490 --> 00:33:44,210
F is the representation.

774
00:33:44,210 --> 00:33:45,390
And then you test right.

775
00:33:45,390 --> 00:33:46,470
That's just regular.

776
00:33:46,470 --> 00:33:50,210
You deploy your system out in the real world.

777
00:33:50,210 --> 00:33:54,130
So I think a lot of you have probably done this because this

778
00:33:54,130 --> 00:33:55,270
is just a practical thing.

779
00:33:55,270 --> 00:33:57,348
If you're ever going to play with deep nets,

780
00:33:57,348 --> 00:33:58,890
you'll download a pre-trained system,

781
00:33:58,890 --> 00:34:00,230
and you'll fine-tune it, right?

782
00:34:00,230 --> 00:34:02,410
So a lot of you I'm sure, have done this,

783
00:34:02,410 --> 00:34:05,470
but it's worth understanding why and how it works.

784
00:34:05,470 --> 00:34:07,330
So fine-tuning-- extremely simple recipe.

785
00:34:07,330 --> 00:34:10,370
Pre-train a network on task A resulting in parameters

786
00:34:10,370 --> 00:34:12,620
w and b, weights and biases.

787
00:34:12,620 --> 00:34:15,219
Those weights and biases are your pre-trained representation

788
00:34:15,219 --> 00:34:16,820
of the data domain.

789
00:34:16,820 --> 00:34:18,659
Then you initialize a second network

790
00:34:18,659 --> 00:34:21,159
using some or all of the weights and biases.

791
00:34:21,159 --> 00:34:23,100
You could initialize some weights

792
00:34:23,100 --> 00:34:26,000
