00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:03,700
just find a way to find a good compression of your data.

1044
00:45:03,700 --> 00:45:05,320
And the other principle is prediction,

1045
00:45:05,320 --> 00:45:08,580
be able to predict missing data when you hold that data out.

1046
00:45:08,580 --> 00:45:10,820
We'll talk about both of these.

1047
00:45:10,820 --> 00:45:13,980
And in the reading, there's a catalog of some of the methods

1048
00:45:13,980 --> 00:45:15,960
we'll see and how at least, in my view,

1049
00:45:15,960 --> 00:45:18,792
they can all be understood as compression or prediction.

1050
00:45:18,792 --> 00:45:20,500
And if you want to, you can actually even

1051
00:45:20,500 --> 00:45:23,360
see the compression prediction as fundamentally the same.

1052
00:45:23,360 --> 00:45:25,360
I'm not sure if there's really a difference,

1053
00:45:25,360 --> 00:45:28,300
but at least it's useful, intuitively,

1054
00:45:28,300 --> 00:45:30,240
to think about compression versus prediction.

1055
00:45:30,240 --> 00:45:32,412
So let's see both of these.

1056
00:45:32,412 --> 00:45:34,120
That's a question for you to think about.

1057
00:45:34,120 --> 00:45:36,203
Is there actually a connection between compression

1058
00:45:36,203 --> 00:45:38,260
and prediction?

1059
00:45:38,260 --> 00:45:41,840
So learning via compression will work as follows.

1060
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:43,580
We'll take our data, and we'll try

1061
00:45:43,580 --> 00:45:46,800
to find that compact mental representation.

1062
00:45:46,800 --> 00:45:50,267
But what is that going to actually do mechanistically?

1063
00:45:50,267 --> 00:45:51,600
So we're going to take our data.

1064
00:45:51,600 --> 00:45:52,800
We're going to find a vector embedding,

1065
00:45:52,800 --> 00:45:55,110
and then we're going to find a lower-dimensional vector

1066
00:45:55,110 --> 00:45:56,510
embedding and a lower-dimensional vector

1067
00:45:56,510 --> 00:45:56,930
embedding.

1068
00:45:56,930 --> 00:45:58,513
And we'll just do that by constructing

1069
00:45:58,513 --> 00:46:02,350
a neural network, where the width decreases

1070
00:46:02,350 --> 00:46:03,370
as a function of depth.

1071
00:46:03,370 --> 00:46:06,790
So each new layer will be lower width.

1072
00:46:06,790 --> 00:46:08,850
So that is all fine.

1073
00:46:08,850 --> 00:46:12,010
I'll learn a low dimensional compressed representation,

1074
00:46:12,010 --> 00:46:14,270
but I also need it to be sufficient in explanatory

1075
00:46:14,270 --> 00:46:14,810
of the data.

1076
00:46:14,810 --> 00:46:18,508
I don't want to just map my input to 0, right?

1077
00:46:18,508 --> 00:46:20,550
I want to map it to something that actually still

1078
00:46:20,550 --> 00:46:22,430
can reconstruct the data.

1079
00:46:22,430 --> 00:46:25,710
So we call that embedding vector-- canonically,

1080
00:46:25,710 --> 00:46:27,590
we'll call it z.

1081
00:46:27,590 --> 00:46:31,310
And we also want to be able to decode

1082
00:46:31,310 --> 00:46:34,150
the image from the compressed representation.

1083
00:46:34,150 --> 00:46:35,670
I'm using a lot of image examples,

1084
00:46:35,670 --> 00:46:37,190
just because I like images.

1085
00:46:37,190 --> 00:46:38,990
I often work on images.

1086
00:46:38,990 --> 00:46:42,270
But think of image as just a placeholder for whatever data

1087
00:46:42,270 --> 00:46:45,242
you want to put there, whatever domain you care about.

1088
00:46:45,242 --> 00:46:46,450
So what is this thing called?

1089
00:46:46,450 --> 00:46:48,430
It's called an autoencoder.

1090
00:46:48,430 --> 00:46:53,390
And this is representation learning algorithm 101,

1091
00:46:53,390 --> 00:46:56,530
just the most basic vanilla one, and also my favorite,

1092
00:46:56,530 --> 00:46:57,670
and probably the best one.

1093
00:46:57,670 --> 00:46:59,833
And I think it will just win out in the end.

1094
00:46:59,833 --> 00:47:01,250
So many things in machine learning

1095
00:47:01,250 --> 00:47:03,490
can be viewed as some kind of autoencoder

1096
00:47:03,490 --> 00:47:05,350
or some variation on an autoencoder.

1097
00:47:05,350 --> 00:47:07,230
So this is a very fundamental concept.

1098
00:47:07,230 --> 00:47:09,850
Just map the data to a simpler form

1099
00:47:09,850 --> 00:47:13,650
such that you can decode the original data from that simpler

1100
00:47:13,650 --> 00:47:15,570
form.

1101
00:47:15,570 --> 00:47:20,610
Autoencoders is just such a beautiful idea.

1102
00:47:20,610 --> 00:47:23,350
This is not the original reference for autoencoders.

1103
00:47:23,350 --> 00:47:27,570
But Hinton, who just won the physics Nobel Prize, of course,

1104
00:47:27,570 --> 00:47:31,290
was one of the people that really helped develop

1105
00:47:31,290 --> 00:47:32,898
autoencoders in his early work.

1106
00:47:32,898 --> 00:47:35,190
I'm not sure there's a single inventor of autoencoders.

1107
00:47:35,190 --> 00:47:36,410
That would probably be--

1108
00:47:36,410 --> 00:47:39,370
yeah, it's an idea that's existed for centuries

1109
00:47:39,370 --> 00:47:42,010
in some form or another.

1110
00:47:42,010 --> 00:47:44,550
So that's an autoencoder.

1111
00:47:44,550 --> 00:47:46,170
So let's look at it mathematically.

1112
00:47:46,170 --> 00:47:48,190
So we have our input data distribution,

1113
00:47:48,190 --> 00:47:49,590
which is all complicated.

1114
00:47:49,590 --> 00:47:51,940
We map it to a compressed, lower-dimensional

1115
00:47:51,940 --> 00:47:53,660
representation.

1116
00:47:53,660 --> 00:47:57,068
So I'm drawing it as a circle because it's a smaller object.

1117
00:47:57,068 --> 00:47:58,360
It's a lower-dimensional thing.

1118
00:47:58,360 --> 00:48:01,620
It might not actually be like a Gaussian object.

1119
00:48:01,620 --> 00:48:03,742
In order for it to actually be a Gaussian circle,

1120
00:48:03,742 --> 00:48:06,200
we'll have to do something called variational autoencoders.

1121
00:48:06,200 --> 00:48:07,242
We'll come to that later.

1122
00:48:07,242 --> 00:48:09,500
But just keep in mind that autoencoders

1123
00:48:09,500 --> 00:48:12,820
do have a way of actually mapping to the circle.

1124
00:48:12,820 --> 00:48:15,860
But we'll talk about just vanilla autoencoders for now.

1125
00:48:15,860 --> 00:48:19,180
And then we'll train the decoder, g,

1126
00:48:19,180 --> 00:48:21,682
which will predict the data in such a way

1127
00:48:21,682 --> 00:48:23,140
that we minimize the reconstruction

1128
00:48:23,140 --> 00:48:26,380
error between the reconstructed predicted data and the input

1129
00:48:26,380 --> 00:48:27,380
data.

1130
00:48:27,380 --> 00:48:28,960
So here's our objective function.

1131
00:48:28,960 --> 00:48:30,700
We're just going to say that encode

1132
00:48:30,700 --> 00:48:32,240
x into a lower-dimensional format,

1133
00:48:32,240 --> 00:48:34,780
decode it back to the original dimensionality

1134
00:48:34,780 --> 00:48:35,860
should be an identity.

1135
00:48:35,860 --> 00:48:37,620
G of f should be an identity.

1136
00:48:37,620 --> 00:48:40,700
And that should just be reconstructing the raw data x

1137
00:48:40,700 --> 00:48:44,700
in expectation over the training set.

1138
00:48:44,700 --> 00:48:49,560
So that's an autoencoder.

1139
00:48:49,560 --> 00:48:55,520
So here's a learning diagram of what the autoencoder objective

1140
00:48:55,520 --> 00:48:56,740
and hypothesis spaces.

1141
00:48:56,740 --> 00:48:59,080
So the critical thing to note here

1142
00:48:59,080 --> 00:49:01,580
is that the autoencoder objective just says,

1143
00:49:01,580 --> 00:49:03,920
I'm going to define big F as being the composition

1144
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:06,640
of the decoder and the encoder.

1145
00:49:06,640 --> 00:49:10,160
So it's encode and then decode becomes this one big function F.

1146
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:12,080
So the funny thing about the autoencoder

1147
00:49:12,080 --> 00:49:15,440
is that this big function F should ideally just

1148
00:49:15,440 --> 00:49:16,620
be an identity.

1149
00:49:16,620 --> 00:49:17,740
It should do nothing.

1150
00:49:17,740 --> 00:49:19,405
So the objective is trivial.

1151
00:49:19,405 --> 00:49:21,780
The objective is you just don't do anything to your data,

1152
00:49:21,780 --> 00:49:25,360
just learn identity.

1153
00:49:25,360 --> 00:49:27,420
If I had no constraints on the architecture,

1154
00:49:27,420 --> 00:49:29,120
on the hypothesis space, the autoencoder

1155
00:49:29,120 --> 00:49:30,760
would be a trivial thing to learn,

1156
00:49:30,760 --> 00:49:31,980
and it would have no utility.

1157
00:49:31,980 --> 00:49:36,600
It would just spit out an exact copy of the data.

1158
00:49:36,600 --> 00:49:38,840
But the key thing is the autoencoder

1159
00:49:38,840 --> 00:49:40,240
works because you put constraints

1160
00:49:40,240 --> 00:49:41,540
over the architecture.

1161
00:49:41,540 --> 00:49:45,017
In our formal language, we'll call it the hypothesis space.

1162
00:49:45,017 --> 00:49:46,600
So the key thing is that we're mapping

1163
00:49:46,600 --> 00:49:50,210
from a high-dimensional space to a lower-dimensional embedding

1164
00:49:50,210 --> 00:49:51,010
and back.

1165
00:49:51,010 --> 00:49:53,290
So typically, m should be less than n.

1166
00:49:53,290 --> 00:49:55,210
Now there are variations on autoencoders

1167
00:49:55,210 --> 00:49:56,710
where you don't need that.

1168
00:49:56,710 --> 00:49:59,930
You can impose simplicity on the embeddings in other ways

1169
00:49:59,930 --> 00:50:01,690
than dimensionality reduction.

1170
00:50:01,690 --> 00:50:04,643
But for vanilla autoencoders, m is less than n.

1171
00:50:04,643 --> 00:50:06,310
And so for an image, it looks like this.

1172
00:50:06,310 --> 00:50:09,970
We have a low-dimensional representation z.

1173
00:50:09,970 --> 00:50:15,530
So question for the audience.

1174
00:50:15,530 --> 00:50:18,753
What if f and g are both linear?

1175
00:50:18,753 --> 00:50:20,170
So I learn a linear transformation

1176
00:50:20,170 --> 00:50:21,753
to a low-dimensional space, and then I

1177
00:50:21,753 --> 00:50:24,530
learn a linear transformation that tries to invert that.

1178
00:50:24,530 --> 00:50:26,470
What do you think that is going to result in?

1179
00:50:26,470 --> 00:50:27,880
Does anybody know?

1180
00:50:27,880 --> 00:50:30,130
It's a model that I've encountered in previous machine

1181
00:50:30,130 --> 00:50:31,150
learning or statistics.

1182
00:50:31,150 --> 00:50:31,650
Yeah?

1183
00:50:31,650 --> 00:50:34,170
AUDIENCE: So will it be another linear?

1184
00:50:34,170 --> 00:50:36,730
PHILLIP ISOLA: That will be another linear function.

1185
00:50:36,730 --> 00:50:39,050
Yeah, that's right.

1186
00:50:39,050 --> 00:50:40,990
OK, yeah, that's one good response.

1187
00:50:40,990 --> 00:50:43,130
Any others?

1188
00:50:43,130 --> 00:50:44,530
Let's go over here.

1189
00:50:44,530 --> 00:50:50,310
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the equivalent of PCA, but--

1190
00:50:50,310 --> 00:50:52,030
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, equivalent of PCA.

1191
00:50:52,030 --> 00:50:52,530
OK, right.

1192
00:50:52,530 --> 00:50:54,990
So you're on to this.

1193
00:50:54,990 --> 00:50:56,050
What is PCA doing?

1194
00:50:56,050 --> 00:51:00,270
PCA can be understood as trying to maximize the variance I'm

1195
00:51:00,270 --> 00:51:05,750
capturing in the signal via some linear orthogonal transformation

1196
00:51:05,750 --> 00:51:07,732
of the vector.

1197
00:51:07,732 --> 00:51:09,190
And if I'm maximizing the variance,

1198
00:51:09,190 --> 00:51:11,230
I'm able to best reconstruct.

1199
00:51:11,230 --> 00:51:15,110
And that actually is exactly equivalent to the L2

1200
00:51:15,110 --> 00:51:16,370
reconstruction objective.

1201
00:51:16,370 --> 00:51:18,830
So PCA can be understood as doing

1202
00:51:18,830 --> 00:51:20,230
essentially the same thing.

1203
00:51:20,230 --> 00:51:21,430
So if they're both linear, we'll just

1204
00:51:21,430 --> 00:51:23,030
replace it with the encoders of matrix

1205
00:51:23,030 --> 00:51:27,710
W. The decoder is a matrix Wg, and the encoders matrix Wf.

1206
00:51:27,710 --> 00:51:30,670
And we're trying to say, if I pass encode, decode,

1207
00:51:30,670 --> 00:51:35,030
I will obtain my original data matrix again.

1208
00:51:35,030 --> 00:51:37,550
That's the objective of an autoencoder

1209
00:51:37,550 --> 00:51:40,630
written with linear f and g.

1210
00:51:40,630 --> 00:51:47,000
And PCA is a variant of this, where we assume that the encoder

1211
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:49,000
and decoder are the same matrix W.

1212
00:51:49,000 --> 00:51:52,720
And they have this property that it's an orthogonal matrix,

1213
00:51:52,720 --> 00:51:54,063
so that's the constraint.

1214
00:51:54,063 --> 00:51:55,480
It's a slightly different setting,

1215
00:51:55,480 --> 00:51:57,080
but it's almost the same.

1216
00:51:57,080 --> 00:52:00,400
And so this is the objective that PCA tries to minimize.

1217
00:52:00,400 --> 00:52:04,400
It tries to find W such that this transformation will

1218
00:52:04,400 --> 00:52:07,760
result in decoding back to the original data.

1219
00:52:07,760 --> 00:52:09,660
So you can work through the math.

1220
00:52:09,660 --> 00:52:11,520
If you're more familiar with PCA maximize

1221
00:52:11,520 --> 00:52:14,560
the variance in the projected space,

1222
00:52:14,560 --> 00:52:20,960
then you can work out that this thing under this condition

1223
00:52:20,960 --> 00:52:25,360
is equivalent to minimize the variance in x

1224
00:52:25,360 --> 00:52:27,960
minus the variance in the transformed version of x.

1225
00:52:27,960 --> 00:52:33,080
And then we can maximize and change the sign

1226
00:52:33,080 --> 00:52:35,200
to be plus instead of minus.

1227
00:52:35,200 --> 00:52:38,080
And then we can remove this term because this term doesn't

1228
00:52:38,080 --> 00:52:40,380
depend on W, and we're maximizing over w.

1229
00:52:40,380 --> 00:52:42,560
And then this is just maximize the variance captured

1230
00:52:42,560 --> 00:52:45,090
in the reconstructions.

1231
00:52:45,090 --> 00:52:50,090
And that might be more familiar PCA that you're used to.

1232
00:52:50,090 --> 00:52:54,570
And what can be proven is that the embeddings

1233
00:52:54,570 --> 00:52:57,010
learned by a linear autoencoder span

1234
00:52:57,010 --> 00:53:00,330
the same subspace as embeddings learned by PCA.

1235
00:53:00,330 --> 00:53:03,210
So this is the rough the rough argument there.

1236
00:53:03,210 --> 00:53:07,690
So vanilla, most intro statistical model

1237
00:53:07,690 --> 00:53:09,650
that you can think of, the most standard one

1238
00:53:09,650 --> 00:53:12,070
that's been around for hundreds of years, I believe,

1239
00:53:12,070 --> 00:53:14,530
or quite a long time, PCA.

1240
00:53:14,530 --> 00:53:16,692
Well, an autoencoder, the very best representation

1241
00:53:16,692 --> 00:53:18,650
learning method in the family of reconstruction

1242
00:53:18,650 --> 00:53:21,290
based representation learning is just nonlinear PCA.

1243
00:53:21,290 --> 00:53:26,650
That's a generalization of PCA to nonlinear representations.

1244
00:53:26,650 --> 00:53:31,650
By the way, I was trying to work out these equations.

1245
00:53:31,650 --> 00:53:36,050
And I found that ChatGPT has gotten incredibly good at this.

1246
00:53:36,050 --> 00:53:37,970
So if you're a little bit confused about--

1247
00:53:37,970 --> 00:53:40,110
I didn't put in every single step here.

1248
00:53:40,110 --> 00:53:42,630
But if you're a little confused, asked

1249
00:53:42,630 --> 00:53:46,550
ChatGPT, what's the connection between PCA

1250
00:53:46,550 --> 00:53:49,790
and linear autoencoders?

1251
00:53:49,790 --> 00:53:52,430
And it will just give this wonderful derivation

1252
00:53:52,430 --> 00:53:53,090
of everything.

1253
00:53:53,090 --> 00:53:54,230
I think it's correct.

1254
00:53:54,230 --> 00:53:55,130
I did check.

1255
00:53:55,130 --> 00:53:56,350
I thought about it.

1256
00:53:56,350 --> 00:53:59,010
But my point is that these tools are getting a lot better.

1257
00:53:59,010 --> 00:54:01,350
So if you're confused about a concept in this class,

1258
00:54:01,350 --> 00:54:03,590
not a homework question, but a concept, just

1259
00:54:03,590 --> 00:54:05,910
ask ChatGPT, or Claude, or whatever language

1260
00:54:05,910 --> 00:54:07,510
model you prefer.

1261
00:54:07,510 --> 00:54:10,670
They're really good at explaining concepts.

1262
00:54:10,670 --> 00:54:13,650
Anyway, of course, they can be wrong.

1263
00:54:13,650 --> 00:54:15,910
You have to be very aware of that.

1264
00:54:15,910 --> 00:54:18,550
But I'm just finding it to be quite a nice resource

1265
00:54:18,550 --> 00:54:19,290
for teaching.

1266
00:54:19,290 --> 00:54:21,310
And I'd recommend it for students, too.

1267
00:54:21,310 --> 00:54:24,093
So let's do a little experiment now.

1268
00:54:24,093 --> 00:54:26,510
Are autoencoder is actually learning good representations?

1269
00:54:26,510 --> 00:54:27,682
Or it sounds nice.

1270
00:54:27,682 --> 00:54:29,890
I learn a low-dimensional, compressed representation.

1271
00:54:29,890 --> 00:54:32,950
But is there any point to doing this besides just compression

1272
00:54:32,950 --> 00:54:34,970
and saving memory?

1273
00:54:34,970 --> 00:54:37,330
OK, obviously, I save memory by doing this.

1274
00:54:37,330 --> 00:54:39,590
I have a smaller object to put on my disk,

1275
00:54:39,590 --> 00:54:41,600
but do I get any other advantage out?

1276
00:54:41,600 --> 00:54:43,210
We said that we think we might get

1277
00:54:43,210 --> 00:54:44,960
representation learning, where we actually

1278
00:54:44,960 --> 00:54:47,360
get a meaningful representation, so do we?

1279
00:54:47,360 --> 00:54:48,380
So here's an experiment.

1280
00:54:48,380 --> 00:54:49,838
You'll do some of these experiments

1281
00:54:49,838 --> 00:54:52,360
on this same type of data on your p set 3.

1282
00:54:52,360 --> 00:54:54,480
So it's a data set of colored shapes.

1283
00:54:54,480 --> 00:54:57,820
We have three different shapes-- triangle, circle, and square.

1284
00:54:57,820 --> 00:55:01,440
And we have nine or so different colors.

1285
00:55:01,440 --> 00:55:05,700
And we're going to try to train an autoencoder on this data.

1286
00:55:05,700 --> 00:55:12,525
So we'll encode some x, and we'll visualize.

1287
00:55:12,525 --> 00:55:13,900
To understand the representation,

1288
00:55:13,900 --> 00:55:16,200
we're going to do this particular probe called

1289
00:55:16,200 --> 00:55:17,840
a nearest neighbor probe.

1290
00:55:17,840 --> 00:55:20,500
We're going to look in the representation space,

1291
00:55:20,500 --> 00:55:22,460
so we're going to embed x into z.

1292
00:55:22,460 --> 00:55:25,720
And then we're going to look at the nearest neighbors to z.

1293
00:55:25,720 --> 00:55:29,160
We're going to look at the images whose z vectors

1294
00:55:29,160 --> 00:55:32,600
are the nearest neighbors to our query image.

1295
00:55:32,600 --> 00:55:34,840
So in the representation space z,

1296
00:55:34,840 --> 00:55:37,250
the nearest neighbors to this triangle

1297
00:55:37,250 --> 00:55:39,000
are going to be these other triangles that

1298
00:55:39,000 --> 00:55:40,620
are roughly the same color.

1299
00:55:40,620 --> 00:55:44,900
And the nearest neighbor to this square will be blue squares.

1300
00:55:44,900 --> 00:55:46,120
So what is this saying?

1301
00:55:46,120 --> 00:55:48,900
This is saying that our representation space

1302
00:55:48,900 --> 00:55:53,100
has organized the data in a way that seems meaningful

1303
00:55:53,100 --> 00:55:54,920
and seems to match human perception.

1304
00:55:54,920 --> 00:55:57,420
So nearby embeddings in representation space,

1305
00:55:57,420 --> 00:55:59,180
actually, are similar images, according

1306
00:55:59,180 --> 00:56:01,160
to their color and their shape.

1307
00:56:01,160 --> 00:56:02,660
So it looks like the autoencoder did

1308
00:56:02,660 --> 00:56:04,940
learn a meaningful representation that

1309
00:56:04,940 --> 00:56:08,500
encodes properties of both color and shape.

1310
00:56:08,500 --> 00:56:10,500
Another thing that we can do is we

1311
00:56:10,500 --> 00:56:14,060
can look at where that representation becomes

1312
00:56:14,060 --> 00:56:14,960
effective.

1313
00:56:14,960 --> 00:56:16,817
So we can now ask--

1314
00:56:16,817 --> 00:56:18,900
we'll measure the quality of the nearest neighbors

1315
00:56:18,900 --> 00:56:21,320
by using them to do classification.

1316
00:56:21,320 --> 00:56:24,425
So we'll do a KNN or, actually, one nearest-neighbor classifier.

1317
00:56:24,425 --> 00:56:26,300
So what is a one nearest-neighbor classifier?

1318
00:56:26,300 --> 00:56:29,020
That just says, is my first nearest neighbor in a data

1319
00:56:29,020 --> 00:56:32,440
set the same class as the query?

1320
00:56:32,440 --> 00:56:35,860
So is it the same color class, and is it the same shape class?

1321
00:56:35,860 --> 00:56:38,430
And I'm plotting accuracy of the one nearest neighbor

1322
00:56:38,430 --> 00:56:41,510
classifier for the shape class and the color class,

1323
00:56:41,510 --> 00:56:46,870
and I'm plotting that at every layer of a encoder.

1324
00:56:46,870 --> 00:56:49,990
This is a convolutional encoder applied to this data.

1325
00:56:49,990 --> 00:56:51,870
So what's going on here?

1326
00:56:51,870 --> 00:56:55,467
The accuracy at shape is getting better,

1327
00:56:55,467 --> 00:56:56,550
which kind of makes sense.

1328
00:56:56,550 --> 00:56:58,508
I'm learning a better and better representation

1329
00:56:58,508 --> 00:57:00,550
so that similar shapes are embedded

1330
00:57:00,550 --> 00:57:02,490
near each other in representation space.

1331
00:57:02,490 --> 00:57:04,330
Why is the accuracy at color getting worse?

1332
00:57:04,330 --> 00:57:05,210
Anyone have an idea?

1333
00:57:05,210 --> 00:57:05,772
Yeah?

1334
00:57:05,772 --> 00:57:07,230
AUDIENCE: In a very crude way, it's

1335
00:57:07,230 --> 00:57:09,990
perhaps learning the principal components of shape

1336
00:57:09,990 --> 00:57:14,072
and separating it from the principal component of color.

1337
00:57:14,072 --> 00:57:16,030
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so it's learning something

1338
00:57:16,030 --> 00:57:19,122
like the one principal component dimension is shape

1339
00:57:19,122 --> 00:57:20,330
and the other is maybe color.

1340
00:57:20,330 --> 00:57:22,470
That would be if I had the linear autoencoder, that

1341
00:57:22,470 --> 00:57:23,968
might be exactly what it does.

1342
00:57:23,968 --> 00:57:25,510
This one's non-linear, but it's going

1343
00:57:25,510 --> 00:57:28,150
to be something kind of like that.

1344
00:57:28,150 --> 00:57:30,990
So anyone have an idea for why color did not

1345
00:57:30,990 --> 00:57:32,730
improve as I go deeper into the network?

1346
00:57:32,730 --> 00:57:34,113
I mean, it got worse.

1347
00:57:34,113 --> 00:57:36,530
But let's just ask the question, why didn't it get better?

1348
00:57:36,530 --> 00:57:37,050
Yeah?

1349
00:57:37,050 --> 00:57:39,810
AUDIENCE: You don't need that much stuff to--

1350
00:57:39,810 --> 00:57:42,410
PHILLIP ISOLA: You don't need that much stuff to decode color.

1351
00:57:42,410 --> 00:57:43,070
That's the answer?

1352
00:57:43,070 --> 00:57:43,590
OK, right.

1353
00:57:43,590 --> 00:57:47,470
So in pixel space, pixel space is a representation of the data.

1354
00:57:47,470 --> 00:57:50,090
It's just the raw format that we take the data in.

1355
00:57:50,090 --> 00:57:53,650
And color is very superficial and explicit in pixel space.

1356
00:57:53,650 --> 00:57:57,037
And the nearest-neighbor image in pixel space to this query

1357
00:57:57,037 --> 00:57:58,370
is going to have the same color.

1358
00:57:58,370 --> 00:58:00,570
Color is explicitly represented in that space.

1359
00:58:00,570 --> 00:58:04,010
Shape is not explicitly represented in pixel space.

1360
00:58:04,010 --> 00:58:09,150
If I do L2 distance between two images represented as pixels,

1361
00:58:09,150 --> 00:58:12,050
just a vector of pixels, it won't

1362
00:58:12,050 --> 00:58:13,510
put similar shapes near each other,

1363
00:58:13,510 --> 00:58:15,850
but it will put similar colors near each other.

1364
00:58:15,850 --> 00:58:19,290
You can think about why that is, but this is the general--

1365
00:58:19,290 --> 00:58:21,970
the general point is that every representation

1366
00:58:21,970 --> 00:58:23,990
is good at some things and bad at other things,

1367
00:58:23,990 --> 00:58:25,170
and there's trade-offs.

1368
00:58:25,170 --> 00:58:28,050
So in this case, the pixels are very good representation

1369
00:58:28,050 --> 00:58:30,450
of color, but a bad representation of shape.

1370
00:58:30,450 --> 00:58:33,110
And the embeddings are a good representation of shape,

1371
00:58:33,110 --> 00:58:34,870
but a worse representation of color,

1372
00:58:34,870 --> 00:58:36,780
in fact, because the pixels were already,

1373
00:58:36,780 --> 00:58:39,380
I guess, the best you could do in this case.

1374
00:58:39,380 --> 00:58:41,437
So autoencoding doesn't strictly result

1375
00:58:41,437 --> 00:58:42,520
in better representations.

1376
00:58:42,520 --> 00:58:44,520
It results in different representations.

1377
00:58:44,520 --> 00:58:45,900
That's a fundamental principle.

1378
00:58:45,900 --> 00:58:47,900
All representation learning is making trade-offs

1379
00:58:47,900 --> 00:58:50,980
to reformat the data into a way that makes some tasks easier,

1380
00:58:50,980 --> 00:58:53,260
shape classification and other tasks harder, color

1381
00:58:53,260 --> 00:58:56,940
classification in this example.

1382
00:58:56,940 --> 00:59:04,740
So I said autoencoders are the deep learning version of PCA.

1383
00:59:04,740 --> 00:59:07,900
And now I'm going to say that another model is

1384
00:59:07,900 --> 00:59:09,720
the deep learning version of k-means.

1385
00:59:09,720 --> 00:59:13,332
So PCA and k-means are the two most important models,

1386
00:59:13,332 --> 00:59:15,540
and everything else is just a generalization of them.

1387
00:59:15,540 --> 00:59:20,053
That's just some opinionated statement.

1388
00:59:20,053 --> 00:59:21,720
But that's one way of thinking about it.

1389
00:59:21,720 --> 00:59:24,380
So let's look at clustering now.

1390
00:59:24,380 --> 00:59:29,380
So clustering is the problem of taking data and assigning

1391
00:59:29,380 --> 00:59:31,500
each data point to a cluster.

1392
00:59:31,500 --> 00:59:35,470
So a representation-learning lens on clustering

1393
00:59:35,470 --> 00:59:38,050
is you're learning an encoder that doesn't output a vector.

1394
00:59:38,050 --> 00:59:39,505
It outputs an integer.

1395
00:59:39,505 --> 00:59:41,630
So for every data point, I output an integer, which

1396
00:59:41,630 --> 00:59:42,910
is the cluster assignment.

1397
00:59:42,910 --> 00:59:44,050
It still is an encoder.

1398
00:59:44,050 --> 00:59:46,390
It's still representation learning.

1399
00:59:46,390 --> 00:59:52,670
So at inference time, if I just apply my learned encoder

1400
00:59:52,670 --> 00:59:57,230
that outputs integers to some data points, it will tell me,

1401
00:59:57,230 --> 00:59:58,230
this is the third class.

1402
00:59:58,230 --> 00:59:59,470
And I'll color that red.

1403
00:59:59,470 --> 01:00:03,710
And that has identified clusters So clustering is learning

1404
01:00:03,710 --> 01:00:07,630
an encoder to integers.

1405
01:00:07,630 --> 01:00:12,190
So why is clustering a good representation?

1406
01:00:12,190 --> 01:00:14,455
So from the representation-learning angle,

1407
01:00:14,455 --> 01:00:15,830
clustering is learning a function

1408
01:00:15,830 --> 01:00:17,538
f that outputs an integer, will represent

1409
01:00:17,538 --> 01:00:19,777
the integers of one-hot code, in this example here.

1410
01:00:19,777 --> 01:00:21,610
So we can think of it as a vector embedding,

1411
01:00:21,610 --> 01:00:23,470
but it's just a one-hot vector embedding

1412
01:00:23,470 --> 01:00:26,530
that's isomorphic with the integers,

1413
01:00:26,530 --> 01:00:29,790
for a finite set of integers.

1414
01:00:29,790 --> 01:00:31,470
So.

1415
01:00:31,470 --> 01:00:33,210
what's the best representation that--

1416
01:00:33,210 --> 01:00:34,890
this is another subjective statement.

1417
01:00:34,890 --> 01:00:36,410
What's the best representation that humans

1418
01:00:36,410 --> 01:00:37,290
have come up with so far?

1419
01:00:37,290 --> 01:00:38,040
What do you think?

1420
01:00:38,040 --> 01:00:40,370
What's the best representation that the human brain

1421
01:00:40,370 --> 01:00:42,630
has discovered?

1422
01:00:42,630 --> 01:00:43,130
Yeah?

1423
01:00:43,130 --> 01:00:43,570
AUDIENCE: Words.

1424
01:00:43,570 --> 01:00:44,612
PHILLIP ISOLA: Words, OK.

1425
01:00:44,612 --> 01:00:46,410
That was my answer.

1426
01:00:46,410 --> 01:00:47,150
You can disagree.

1427
01:00:47,150 --> 01:00:48,530
I don't know if this is true.

1428
01:00:48,530 --> 01:00:51,710
Traditionally, I come from a computer vision background,

1429
01:00:51,710 --> 01:00:55,102
and so this is kind of blasphemy, I suppose.

1430
01:00:55,102 --> 01:00:57,810
But I think language is just the best representation of the world

1431
01:00:57,810 --> 01:00:59,190
that humans have discovered.

1432
01:00:59,190 --> 01:01:00,607
There's other representations that

1433
01:01:00,607 --> 01:01:02,110
are good and have their trade-offs,

1434
01:01:02,110 --> 01:01:04,090
but language is like the pinnacle.

1435
01:01:04,090 --> 01:01:06,290
And words are the atoms of language.

1436
01:01:06,290 --> 01:01:08,590
There's grammar and there's structures between words,

1437
01:01:08,590 --> 01:01:11,490
but words are the important component of language.

1438
01:01:11,490 --> 01:01:14,560
And clustering is the problem of making up new words for things.

1439
01:01:14,560 --> 01:01:16,310
That's one way of understanding it, right?

1440
01:01:16,310 --> 01:01:21,170
A word is like a symbol that at least--

1441
01:01:21,170 --> 01:01:23,670
there might be some linguists that would quibble about this,

1442
01:01:23,670 --> 01:01:26,410
but one rough definition of word is

1443
01:01:26,410 --> 01:01:29,110
it's just like it is a symbol that denotes a set,

1444
01:01:29,110 --> 01:01:31,070
and that's what clustering is doing.

1445
01:01:31,070 --> 01:01:34,860
So this is like saying, oh, I will map every image

1446
01:01:34,860 --> 01:01:38,780
like this to this cluster, and I can just label it arbitrarily

1447
01:01:38,780 --> 01:01:40,940
with a word.

1448
01:01:40,940 --> 01:01:42,840
Language has more structure beyond the words,

1449
01:01:42,840 --> 01:01:45,120
but this is but words are an important part of it.

1450
01:01:45,120 --> 01:01:46,860
So clustering is the representation

1451
01:01:46,860 --> 01:01:51,900
learning problem of finding new words for concepts.

1452
01:01:51,900 --> 01:01:53,500
Let's talk about k-means.

1453
01:01:53,500 --> 01:01:54,960
So this should be review.

1454
01:01:54,960 --> 01:01:56,760
I hope you've seen PCA and k-means before.

1455
01:01:56,760 --> 01:01:58,593
So if you haven't, go back and review those.

1456
01:01:58,593 --> 01:02:01,420
Because as I said, deep learning is just generalizing those,

1457
01:02:01,420 --> 01:02:04,360
representation learning is generalizing these concepts.

1458
01:02:04,360 --> 01:02:08,980
So what does k-means do? k-means finds k different clusters

1459
01:02:08,980 --> 01:02:10,060
in your data.

1460
01:02:10,060 --> 01:02:12,340
And each cluster is represented with what's

1461
01:02:12,340 --> 01:02:15,100
called the mean, which is just going

1462
01:02:15,100 --> 01:02:18,100
to be the average of all the data points in that cluster.

1463
01:02:18,100 --> 01:02:20,420
So it maps data points to integers.

1464
01:02:20,420 --> 01:02:22,780
And it does that in such a way that each data point is

1465
01:02:22,780 --> 01:02:26,220
as close as possible to the mean of the data

1466
01:02:26,220 --> 01:02:30,920
points in the cluster it is assigned to.

1467
01:02:30,920 --> 01:02:36,040
So here's the representation learning view of k-means.

1468
01:02:36,040 --> 01:02:38,900
I'm going to train an encoder that will output one-hot codes.

1469
01:02:38,900 --> 01:02:42,647
And then my decoder will just be--

1470
01:02:42,647 --> 01:02:43,980
think of it like a lookup table.

1471
01:02:43,980 --> 01:02:46,360
It takes in a one-hot code, and it outputs a vector.

1472
01:02:46,360 --> 01:02:48,840
And it's trying to output the vector that will reconstruct

1473
01:02:48,840 --> 01:02:51,960
the input as best as possible.

1474
01:02:51,960 --> 01:02:54,640
If I only can output a single vector

1475
01:02:54,640 --> 01:02:57,560
for every item in a cluster, every item in the cluster

1476
01:02:57,560 --> 01:02:59,300
goes to the same one-hot code.

1477
01:02:59,300 --> 01:03:02,320
So now I can decode that into just a single vector.

1478
01:03:02,320 --> 01:03:05,100
Think of g as a matrix W applied to a one-hot code.

1479
01:03:05,100 --> 01:03:08,240
It selects a row of that matrix.

1480
01:03:08,240 --> 01:03:14,480
What is going to be the decoder that minimizes the Euclidean

1481
01:03:14,480 --> 01:03:18,340
distance reconstruction error, the L2 reconstruction error?

1482
01:03:18,340 --> 01:03:20,240
What is the name for the vector that

1483
01:03:20,240 --> 01:03:23,920
minimizes the distance to all points

1484
01:03:23,920 --> 01:03:27,060
in a set, the L2 distance?

1485
01:03:27,060 --> 01:03:27,560
Yeah?

1486
01:03:27,560 --> 01:03:28,050
AUDIENCE: Mean.

1487
01:03:28,050 --> 01:03:28,970
PHILLIP ISOLA: The mean, OK.

1488
01:03:28,970 --> 01:03:30,928
I think a lot of that, maybe some of you don't.

1489
01:03:30,928 --> 01:03:34,792
But, yes, the vector that minimizes the L2 distance

1490
01:03:34,792 --> 01:03:37,250
to a set of other points is the mean of those other points.

1491
01:03:37,250 --> 01:03:38,890
You can show that.

1492
01:03:38,890 --> 01:03:42,933
So that means that k-means is an L2 autoencoder.

1493
01:03:42,933 --> 01:03:44,850
The only difference from the other autoencoder

1494
01:03:44,850 --> 01:03:47,582
that I showed you is that the hypothesis space doesn't have

1495
01:03:47,582 --> 01:03:48,790
a low-dimensional bottleneck.

1496
01:03:48,790 --> 01:03:53,050
It has an integer bottleneck, OK?

1497
01:03:53,050 --> 01:03:57,550
So that's just a mapping of k-means onto autoencoders.

1498
01:03:57,550 --> 01:04:00,570
They're the same model with a different hypothesis space.

1499
01:04:00,570 --> 01:04:03,190
k-means, this hypothesis space is not differentiable.

1500
01:04:03,190 --> 01:04:05,448
It has some properties that can be exploited,

1501
01:04:05,448 --> 01:04:06,990
some structure that can be exploited.

1502
01:04:06,990 --> 01:04:10,710
So we optimize it with a different algorithm than SGD.

1503
01:04:10,710 --> 01:04:13,690
And that's where you might have encountered the expectation

1504
01:04:13,690 --> 01:04:17,030
maximization method for doing k-means and so forth.

1505
01:04:17,030 --> 01:04:20,490
And, yeah, that's just because this problem

1506
01:04:20,490 --> 01:04:21,910
has some special structure.

1507
01:04:26,230 --> 01:04:28,590
And then the deep learning version of k-means

1508
01:04:28,590 --> 01:04:31,670
is called a vector quantized autoencoder.

1509
01:04:31,670 --> 01:04:33,870
So a vector quantized autoencoder is exactly

1510
01:04:33,870 --> 01:04:36,070
the same as k-means, except that f is

1511
01:04:36,070 --> 01:04:38,270
non-linear instead of linear.

1512
01:04:38,270 --> 01:04:41,090
And there's a whole bunch of variations on VQ models.

1513
01:04:41,090 --> 01:04:43,670
There's VQGAN, VQVAE, et cetera, et cetera.

1514
01:04:43,670 --> 01:04:46,130
These have bells and whistles, but this is the gist of it.

1515
01:04:46,130 --> 01:04:49,810
I'm trying to learn an encoding into a set of integers

1516
01:04:49,810 --> 01:04:52,270
and a decoding that minimizes reconstruction error.

1517
01:04:52,270 --> 01:04:56,950
And f will be a deep network, or little f in little g

1518
01:04:56,950 --> 01:05:00,750
are both going to be deep neural networks.

1519
01:05:00,750 --> 01:05:03,130
So you can read up on exactly how that method works,

1520
01:05:03,130 --> 01:05:07,870
but it's just k-means with deep nets.

1521
01:05:07,870 --> 01:05:08,590
Yeah?

1522
01:05:08,590 --> 01:05:10,670
AUDIENCE: In this k-means analogy,

1523
01:05:10,670 --> 01:05:13,090
does the autoencoder learn to find the best k?

1524
01:05:15,950 --> 01:05:16,730
PHILLIP ISOLA: No.

1525
01:05:16,730 --> 01:05:18,890
So does the autoencoder learn to find the best k's?

1526
01:05:18,890 --> 01:05:21,830
So k is a hyperparameter and, typically, the user

1527
01:05:21,830 --> 01:05:24,510
has to define it.

1528
01:05:24,510 --> 01:05:27,800
Yeah, you can have methods that try to automatically determine

1529
01:05:27,800 --> 01:05:31,040
k, but that's beyond the scope of these vanilla methods.

1530
01:05:34,840 --> 01:05:37,000
So data compression-- that was the first way

1531
01:05:37,000 --> 01:05:39,640
of learning representations.

1532
01:05:39,640 --> 01:05:46,000
And it shows up in PCA, and k-means, and VQVAEs, and VQGANs,

1533
01:05:46,000 --> 01:05:49,065
and autoencoders, and so forth.

1534
01:05:49,065 --> 01:05:50,440
There's another thing you can do,

1535
01:05:50,440 --> 01:05:53,060
and that will be the prediction method.

1536
01:05:53,060 --> 01:05:55,520
So now rather than learning representations

1537
01:05:55,520 --> 01:05:58,680
by compressing data, we're going to try to learn representations

1538
01:05:58,680 --> 01:06:02,040
by predicting held-out data.

1539
01:06:02,040 --> 01:06:04,885
And this is actually the kind I started this lecture with.

1540
01:06:04,885 --> 01:06:06,760
I said that we could just pre-train a network

1541
01:06:06,760 --> 01:06:08,420
on music classification.

1542
01:06:08,420 --> 01:06:11,280
That's a prediction problem, and it induces an OK representation

1543
01:06:11,280 --> 01:06:13,820
for music classification.

1544
01:06:13,820 --> 01:06:16,320
But we're instead going to do something else, which is we'll

1545
01:06:16,320 --> 01:06:17,420
say, we didn't want labels.

1546
01:06:17,420 --> 01:06:19,712
We don't want to be we don't want a representation that

1547
01:06:19,712 --> 01:06:21,395
is tied to a specific task.

1548
01:06:21,395 --> 01:06:24,020
So we don't want to have to have humans label some narrow task.

1549
01:06:24,020 --> 01:06:26,090
We do something much more generic

1550
01:06:26,090 --> 01:06:29,930
that doesn't require annotations or task specificity.

1551
01:06:29,930 --> 01:06:31,583
And one way to do that is we say, we're

1552
01:06:31,583 --> 01:06:32,750
not going to predict labels.

1553
01:06:32,750 --> 01:06:35,570
We're going to predict the raw data itself.

1554
01:06:35,570 --> 01:06:38,030
And this is called self-supervised learning.

1555
01:06:38,030 --> 01:06:39,677
So it's called self-supervised learning

1556
01:06:39,677 --> 01:06:42,010
because it's using the machinery of supervised learning,

1557
01:06:42,010 --> 01:06:45,690
meaning predict y from x, except that we define y

1558
01:06:45,690 --> 01:06:50,510
as being some part of the raw data as opposed to some label.

1559
01:06:50,510 --> 01:06:52,810
So this is like an autoencoder, except I'm

1560
01:06:52,810 --> 01:06:56,810
predicting half of the data from the other half of the data.

1561
01:06:56,810 --> 01:07:00,230
And interestingly, this works really well.

1562
01:07:00,230 --> 01:07:03,170
This tends to work a lot better than autoencoders.

1563
01:07:03,170 --> 01:07:05,822
So here's an example of this setup.

1564
01:07:05,822 --> 01:07:08,030
This is the colorization problem I showed you before.

1565
01:07:08,030 --> 01:07:10,890
Try to predict the colors from a black and white image?

1566
01:07:10,890 --> 01:07:13,238
And this is a self-supervised method

1567
01:07:13,238 --> 01:07:15,030
that tries to predict part of the raw data,

1568
01:07:15,030 --> 01:07:17,280
the color channels, from another part of the raw data,

1569
01:07:17,280 --> 01:07:20,070
the black and white channel.

1570
01:07:20,070 --> 01:07:22,790
So we can now take that model that's

1571
01:07:22,790 --> 01:07:25,590
just trained to do this self-supervised prediction

1572
01:07:25,590 --> 01:07:26,950
of the missing colors.

1573
01:07:26,950 --> 01:07:30,510
Now it's free labels because color images

1574
01:07:30,510 --> 01:07:32,163
have the colors built in.

1575
01:07:32,163 --> 01:07:33,330
I just took the color image.

1576
01:07:33,330 --> 01:07:35,872
I split it into the luminance channel and the color channels.

1577
01:07:35,872 --> 01:07:37,410
So it's cheap.

1578
01:07:37,410 --> 01:07:40,790
It's easy to run this on just raw, unlabeled data.

1579
01:07:40,790 --> 01:07:43,310
Now I can investigate, did this system actually learn

1580
01:07:43,310 --> 01:07:44,658
a meaningful representation?

1581
01:07:44,658 --> 01:07:46,450
So we'll do the deep net electrophysiology.

1582
01:07:46,450 --> 01:07:48,158
I'll poke my probe in there and ask, what

1583
01:07:48,158 --> 01:07:52,230
are the neurons sensitive to?

1584
01:07:52,230 --> 01:07:55,430
So here, I guess I'll ask the class.

1585
01:07:55,430 --> 01:07:56,830
What do you think?

1586
01:07:56,830 --> 01:08:00,530
Remember when I talked about the Zeiler and Fergus model,

1587
01:08:00,530 --> 01:08:03,030
I said that they were like face selective neurons,

1588
01:08:03,030 --> 01:08:05,830
and dog face selective neurons, and other things.

1589
01:08:05,830 --> 01:08:08,550
What do you think the neurons will be sensitive to

1590
01:08:08,550 --> 01:08:09,980
for predicting colors?

1591
01:08:09,980 --> 01:08:11,730
And let's look at layer five of a network,

1592
01:08:11,730 --> 01:08:14,590
so deep inside the network.

1593
01:08:14,590 --> 01:08:15,570
What do you all think?

1594
01:08:19,029 --> 01:08:19,838
Yeah, over here?

1595
01:08:19,838 --> 01:08:20,630
AUDIENCE: Textures.

1596
01:08:20,630 --> 01:08:22,047
PHILLIP ISOLA: Textures, OK, good.

1597
01:08:22,047 --> 01:08:23,175
Yeah, textures seem to be--

1598
01:08:23,175 --> 01:08:24,300
color is kind of low level.

1599
01:08:24,300 --> 01:08:25,200
It seems like it should be something

1600
01:08:25,200 --> 01:08:27,340
that's a photometric property, like texture.

1601
01:08:27,340 --> 01:08:28,729
Any other ideas?

1602
01:08:28,729 --> 01:08:29,479
Yeah, in the back.

1603
01:08:29,479 --> 01:08:30,375
AUDIENCE: Objects.

1604
01:08:30,375 --> 01:08:31,500
PHILLIP ISOLA: Objects, OK.

1605
01:08:31,500 --> 01:08:34,960
Yeah, so why objects?

1606
01:08:34,960 --> 01:08:36,880
AUDIENCE: Different classes of objects

1607
01:08:36,880 --> 01:08:39,237
have different colors like [INAUDIBLE]

1608
01:08:39,237 --> 01:08:41,279
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, different classes of objects

1609
01:08:41,279 --> 01:08:42,720
have different colors.

1610
01:08:42,720 --> 01:08:44,120
If I know something's strawberry,

1611
01:08:44,120 --> 01:08:45,500
I can say it's probably red.

1612
01:08:45,500 --> 01:08:48,760
So knowing the object category tells me a lot about the color.

1613
01:08:48,760 --> 01:08:49,660
Anything else?

1614
01:08:53,000 --> 01:08:58,500
So, yes, it turns out objects is one of the answers.

1615
01:08:58,500 --> 01:09:01,240
So here are our three different neurons.

1616
01:09:01,240 --> 01:09:03,960
And this is looking at the feature maps.

1617
01:09:03,960 --> 01:09:06,520
And we're just shading-- we're blacking out

1618
01:09:06,520 --> 01:09:08,120
all the parts of the feature maps

1619
01:09:08,120 --> 01:09:10,260
where the activations are below a threshold.

1620
01:09:10,260 --> 01:09:12,279
So this is like, where are the neurons firing

1621
01:09:12,279 --> 01:09:15,760
at some convolutional layer 5 of this network?

1622
01:09:15,760 --> 01:09:17,920
And, yeah, on lower layers, it is textures

1623
01:09:17,920 --> 01:09:19,060
and other things like this.

1624
01:09:19,060 --> 01:09:21,620
But the interesting thing is that it doesn't really matter

1625
01:09:21,620 --> 01:09:22,880
how you train these networks.

1626
01:09:22,880 --> 01:09:25,140
If you train them to predict classes,

1627
01:09:25,140 --> 01:09:26,640
if you train them to predict colors,

1628
01:09:26,640 --> 01:09:28,478
if you train them to inpaint missing pixels,

1629
01:09:28,478 --> 01:09:30,020
just pick the right half of the image

1630
01:09:30,020 --> 01:09:33,740
from the left half, the units that

1631
01:09:33,740 --> 01:09:35,859
carve the world at its joints, that are predictive

1632
01:09:35,859 --> 01:09:39,268
of everything, turn out to be objects and semantics

1633
01:09:39,268 --> 01:09:40,560
and the words that humans have.

1634
01:09:40,560 --> 01:09:41,840
So it's like words are not arbitrary.

1635
01:09:41,840 --> 01:09:43,548
We have the words we have because they're

1636
01:09:43,548 --> 01:09:46,779
very predictive statistically of missing data.

1637
01:09:46,779 --> 01:09:49,859
So this is discovery of semantic words

1638
01:09:49,859 --> 01:09:53,160
without any semantic labels.

1639
01:09:53,160 --> 01:09:54,620
And this has been the big finding

1640
01:09:54,620 --> 01:09:57,900
over the last decade that led to this revolution in how

1641
01:09:57,900 --> 01:09:59,620
we do deep learning, which was the move

1642
01:09:59,620 --> 01:10:02,340
from supervised learning to self-supervised learning.

1643
01:10:02,340 --> 01:10:04,603
So the self-supervised learning is--

1644
01:10:04,603 --> 01:10:06,020
we used to try to do what's called

1645
01:10:06,020 --> 01:10:08,420
unsupervised learning, which is just

1646
01:10:08,420 --> 01:10:10,160
learn from raw, unlabeled data.

1647
01:10:10,160 --> 01:10:13,500
But then we switch it into the mathematical machinery

1648
01:10:13,500 --> 01:10:17,740
of supervised learning by just predicting some fake labels,

1649
01:10:17,740 --> 01:10:19,990
which are just raw data, which you call pretext labels

1650
01:10:19,990 --> 01:10:23,630
or pretext tasks from the data itself.

1651
01:10:23,630 --> 01:10:25,670
And there's so many different ways

1652
01:10:25,670 --> 01:10:27,790
of doing this learning by prediction.

1653
01:10:27,790 --> 01:10:29,430
So you could just predict class labels.

1654
01:10:29,430 --> 01:10:32,310
That would be called supervised learning of representations.

1655
01:10:32,310 --> 01:10:35,690
You could predict the next frame in a video, the future.

1656
01:10:35,690 --> 01:10:38,910
You could predict the next pixel in a sequence and the same thing

1657
01:10:38,910 --> 01:10:41,550
for any other data modality, too.

1658
01:10:41,550 --> 01:10:44,990
So the two on the right are self-supervised because no human

1659
01:10:44,990 --> 01:10:47,012
had to provide the label target.

1660
01:10:47,012 --> 01:10:48,970
It's the next frame in the video, the raw data.

1661
01:10:48,970 --> 01:10:51,710
It's the next pixel in a sequence.

1662
01:10:51,710 --> 01:10:54,470
And as you probably know, the way that language models work

1663
01:10:54,470 --> 01:10:57,330
is they predict the next word in a sequence,

1664
01:10:57,330 --> 01:11:01,110
so they're mostly in the family of this type of learning.

1665
01:11:01,110 --> 01:11:03,910
And most people still call language models

1666
01:11:03,910 --> 01:11:07,570
self-supervised because they're just predicting the raw data.

1667
01:11:07,570 --> 01:11:09,770
It happens the raw data is semantic and is words,

1668
01:11:09,770 --> 01:11:11,888
but in that context, it's not like they're

1669
01:11:11,888 --> 01:11:12,930
predicting the sentiment.

1670
01:11:12,930 --> 01:11:15,310
They're just predicting the next word.

1671
01:11:15,310 --> 01:11:19,610
So all of these self-supervised tasks

1672
01:11:19,610 --> 01:11:22,610
can be understood as something we call imputation, which just

1673
01:11:22,610 --> 01:11:26,450
means take your data-- it could be a matrix, a tensor, some data

1674
01:11:26,450 --> 01:11:26,990
object.

1675
01:11:26,990 --> 01:11:29,470
A video would be like time by x by y.

1676
01:11:29,470 --> 01:11:32,590
A sentence would be time by the content at that time,

1677
01:11:32,590 --> 01:11:34,090
the word at that time.

1678
01:11:34,090 --> 01:11:39,210
And mask part of it, and put that into an encoder.

1679
01:11:39,210 --> 01:11:42,530
Decode it to predict the masked part of that input.

1680
01:11:42,530 --> 01:11:44,490
So masked prediction or imputation

1681
01:11:44,490 --> 01:11:48,370
is the standard pretext tasks that people

1682
01:11:48,370 --> 01:11:51,090
like to use these days to learn representations.

1683
01:11:51,090 --> 01:11:55,690
So spatial imputation, just predict the next pixel

1684
01:11:55,690 --> 01:11:56,750
from the previous pixel.

1685
01:11:56,750 --> 01:11:58,583
Temporal imputation-- predict the next frame

1686
01:11:58,583 --> 01:12:00,090
from the previous frame.

1687
01:12:00,090 --> 01:12:03,930
Channel imputation-- predict the colors from the black and white

1688
01:12:03,930 --> 01:12:06,150
and, again, on other modalities, you can do the same.

1689
01:12:11,050 --> 01:12:14,123
Yeah, you can do this in a way that frames it

1690
01:12:14,123 --> 01:12:16,540
just like an autoencoder, again, but it will be called now

1691
01:12:16,540 --> 01:12:17,640
a masked autoencoder.

1692
01:12:17,640 --> 01:12:22,220
So masked autoencoder is you take your data,

1693
01:12:22,220 --> 01:12:25,660
you mask random chunks of it.

1694
01:12:25,660 --> 01:12:29,460
And the little interesting trick is that in masked autoencoder

1695
01:12:29,460 --> 01:12:31,340
is applied to images.

1696
01:12:31,340 --> 01:12:33,240
You're using a vision transformer.

1697
01:12:33,240 --> 01:12:34,900
And the vision transformer already

1698
01:12:34,900 --> 01:12:37,705
tokenized the image into the first-- remember,

1699
01:12:37,705 --> 01:12:39,080
we talked about vision transform.

1700
01:12:39,080 --> 01:12:41,380
We said that we are going to create a set of tokens

1701
01:12:41,380 --> 01:12:43,060
by chopping the image into patches

1702
01:12:43,060 --> 01:12:47,140
and then mapping those patches to vector embeddings or tokens

1703
01:12:47,140 --> 01:12:48,188
that then get processed.

1704
01:12:48,188 --> 01:12:49,980
And so they just said, well, what if I just

1705
01:12:49,980 --> 01:12:51,700
remove some of the tokens, and then I

1706
01:12:51,700 --> 01:12:52,840
predict the missing tokens?

1707
01:12:52,840 --> 01:12:58,140
And the really interesting thing about the attention architecture

1708
01:12:58,140 --> 01:13:01,640
is that I can mask different ratios.

1709
01:13:01,640 --> 01:13:04,200
I can only keep four of these tokens,

1710
01:13:04,200 --> 01:13:07,300
but then the attention mechanism will

1711
01:13:07,300 --> 01:13:09,500
scale in a way that is proportional to the number

1712
01:13:09,500 --> 01:13:10,368
of tokens.

1713
01:13:10,368 --> 01:13:12,160
Each token will attend to each other token.

1714
01:13:12,160 --> 01:13:13,880
So if I have four, it will be four attending to four.

1715
01:13:13,880 --> 01:13:15,700
And it will output predictions for four.

1716
01:13:15,700 --> 01:13:17,908
If I have eight, it will be eight attending to eight.

1717
01:13:17,908 --> 01:13:21,160
So it has this nice kind of architectural invariance

1718
01:13:21,160 --> 01:13:23,560
to the number of tokens you put in.

1719
01:13:23,560 --> 01:13:24,960
And then I decode.

1720
01:13:24,960 --> 01:13:28,360
I just have some blank tokens I put into another transformer.

1721
01:13:28,360 --> 01:13:31,760
And then I have trained it so that those blank tokens get

1722
01:13:31,760 --> 01:13:36,920
filled in with the prediction of the pixels in the missing

1723
01:13:36,920 --> 01:13:39,800
tokens, or the prediction of the data in the missing tokens.

1724
01:13:39,800 --> 01:13:42,440
And masked autoencoders are just a new name

1725
01:13:42,440 --> 01:13:47,440
for another model which was very popular called BERT.

1726
01:13:47,440 --> 01:13:49,357
So there are differences.

1727
01:13:49,357 --> 01:13:51,440
Engineering this to work on language versus vision

1728
01:13:51,440 --> 01:13:53,815
is a very important difference but, conceptually, they're

1729
01:13:53,815 --> 01:13:55,040
almost the same thing.

1730
01:13:55,040 --> 01:13:57,640
So BERT was a language model that was very popular

1731
01:13:57,640 --> 01:13:58,760
some years ago.

1732
01:13:58,760 --> 01:14:01,320
It's gone a bit out of fashion, but BERT is

1733
01:14:01,320 --> 01:14:04,220
roughly the same thing on text.

1734
01:14:04,220 --> 01:14:05,420
So I just take my text.

1735
01:14:05,420 --> 01:14:06,140
I tokenize it.

1736
01:14:06,140 --> 01:14:07,620
I mask some of the tokens.

1737
01:14:07,620 --> 01:14:10,500
I run it through a transformer, and I predict all the tokens.

1738
01:14:10,500 --> 01:14:14,130
So I'm now doing masked prediction with language.

1739
01:14:14,130 --> 01:14:18,250
And the autoregressive models that

1740
01:14:18,250 --> 01:14:20,210
try to predict the next word in a sentence

1741
01:14:20,210 --> 01:14:21,850
are just the same, except they're only

1742
01:14:21,850 --> 01:14:24,730
masking the final word as opposed to interleaving words.

1743
01:14:24,730 --> 01:14:26,450
And that has some advantages in that

1744
01:14:26,450 --> 01:14:29,450
I can decode in sequential order as opposed

1745
01:14:29,450 --> 01:14:33,530
to in out-of-time order.

1746
01:14:33,530 --> 01:14:36,130
So, yeah, question?

1747
01:14:36,130 --> 01:14:38,587
AUDIENCE: Why is BERT getting out of fashion?

1748
01:14:38,587 --> 01:14:40,670
PHILLIP ISOLA: Why is BERT getting out of fashion?

1749
01:14:40,670 --> 01:14:46,210
So I think it's because masking the final token, naturally,

1750
01:14:46,210 --> 01:14:49,810
can be used for generating sentences autoregressively,

1751
01:14:49,810 --> 01:14:51,690
which we talked about before.

1752
01:14:51,690 --> 01:14:53,690
If I mask the interleaving, then it's

1753
01:14:53,690 --> 01:14:57,310
like I'm generating words out of temporal order.

1754
01:14:57,310 --> 01:14:59,730
So if I'm talking with a human, time

1755
01:14:59,730 --> 01:15:03,630
is an axis that is important and not symmetric with other axes.

1756
01:15:03,630 --> 01:15:06,450
I have to answer the question after the question has

1757
01:15:06,450 --> 01:15:08,650
been asked by the person I'm talking to.

1758
01:15:08,650 --> 01:15:11,580
And so having these causal attention

1759
01:15:11,580 --> 01:15:13,720
mechanisms for the masking is in causal order.

1760
01:15:13,720 --> 01:15:16,320
I'm only masking the future in conditioning on the past.

1761
01:15:16,320 --> 01:15:18,360
It just fits into language models,

1762
01:15:18,360 --> 01:15:20,420
which operate in causal order.

1763
01:15:20,420 --> 01:15:22,980
And once that became popular, the biggest models

1764
01:15:22,980 --> 01:15:26,500
were all masking only the tokens in the future, given

1765
01:15:26,500 --> 01:15:27,900
the tokens in the past.

1766
01:15:27,900 --> 01:15:30,077
And because those were the biggest models,

1767
01:15:30,077 --> 01:15:31,160
they just worked the best.

1768
01:15:31,160 --> 01:15:32,960
But if I want to learn a sentence embedding,

1769
01:15:32,960 --> 01:15:34,140
I bet the BERT method is still going

1770
01:15:34,140 --> 01:15:35,848
to work better if scaled the same amount.

1771
01:15:40,140 --> 01:15:44,100
OK, so here's an interesting empirical finding.

1772
01:15:44,100 --> 01:15:46,120
This is going back to that colorization paper,

1773
01:15:46,120 --> 01:15:48,600
but it's been shown in a lot of work,

1774
01:15:48,600 --> 01:15:53,060
which is that masked prediction just tends to always work better

1775
01:15:53,060 --> 01:15:55,360
than autoencoding.

1776
01:15:55,360 --> 01:15:57,720
So if I I'm going to look at the accuracy,

1777
01:15:57,720 --> 01:16:00,460
I'm going to look at the representations

1778
01:16:00,460 --> 01:16:04,020
at each layer of an autoencoder versus a masked prediction

1779
01:16:04,020 --> 01:16:06,120
network, for colorization in this case.

1780
01:16:06,120 --> 01:16:09,740
And I'm going to assess performance

1781
01:16:09,740 --> 01:16:11,560
seeing how well I can linearly classify

1782
01:16:11,560 --> 01:16:13,400
given the representation on that layer.

1783
01:16:13,400 --> 01:16:15,700
Imagenet categories, in this case, is like a probe,

1784
01:16:15,700 --> 01:16:17,520
just like we were doing with the shapes, where we had the nearest

1785
01:16:17,520 --> 01:16:18,145
neighbor probe.

1786
01:16:18,145 --> 01:16:20,200
Now we have a linear classifier probe.

1787
01:16:20,200 --> 01:16:21,220
So here's what happens.

1788
01:16:21,220 --> 01:16:22,760
As I go deeper in the network, you

1789
01:16:22,760 --> 01:16:24,960
get this separation, where autoencoding

1790
01:16:24,960 --> 01:16:27,960
learns an OK representation, but masked

1791
01:16:27,960 --> 01:16:30,120
prediction learns a representation which

1792
01:16:30,120 --> 01:16:31,180
is more semantic.

1793
01:16:31,180 --> 01:16:35,280
It linearly decodes ImageNet categories better.

1794
01:16:35,280 --> 01:16:38,880
And this is something that I thought I had a good explanation

1795
01:16:38,880 --> 01:16:40,935
for, and then I realized I don't.

1796
01:16:40,935 --> 01:16:42,560
So I'm going to call it ongoing science

1797
01:16:42,560 --> 01:16:44,700
and leave it as a puzzle for the class.

1798
01:16:44,700 --> 01:16:46,680
And maybe this would be a good final project.

1799
01:16:46,680 --> 01:16:50,840
Why does masked prediction work better than autoencoding?

1800
01:16:50,840 --> 01:16:52,740
So here are three hypotheses to think about.

1801
01:16:52,740 --> 01:16:57,960
So one is that autoencoding controls compression

1802
01:16:57,960 --> 01:17:00,280
via dimensional bottleneck.

1803
01:17:00,280 --> 01:17:03,640
Masked prediction controls compression

1804
01:17:03,640 --> 01:17:07,040
by the non-overlap between the outputs you're predicting

1805
01:17:07,040 --> 01:17:10,033
and the inputs you're conditioning on.

1806
01:17:10,033 --> 01:17:12,450
So the only thing that's useful for predicting the outputs

1807
01:17:12,450 --> 01:17:14,283
is the mutual information between the inputs

1808
01:17:14,283 --> 01:17:14,950
and the outputs.

1809
01:17:14,950 --> 01:17:16,428
And if these are separate, then I

1810
01:17:16,428 --> 01:17:17,970
will just forget all the stuff that's

1811
01:17:17,970 --> 01:17:21,190
specific to the inputs that's not relevant for the output.

1812
01:17:21,190 --> 01:17:23,950
So that's how it does compression.

1813
01:17:23,950 --> 01:17:26,370
So it could be that just controlling compression

1814
01:17:26,370 --> 01:17:29,330
via dimensional bottlenecks is really hard to make work.

1815
01:17:29,330 --> 01:17:30,190
It's very finicky.

1816
01:17:30,190 --> 01:17:31,715
It requires an architecture that has

1817
01:17:31,715 --> 01:17:33,090
constraints on the dimensionality

1818
01:17:33,090 --> 01:17:34,250
of the embeddings.

1819
01:17:34,250 --> 01:17:37,470
And we know that low-dimensional things,

1820
01:17:37,470 --> 01:17:41,130
anything with low dimension and deep learning just is hard.

1821
01:17:41,130 --> 01:17:44,890
It interacts with optimization in weird ways and interacts

1822
01:17:44,890 --> 01:17:47,070
with BatchNorm and LayerNorm in weird ways.

1823
01:17:47,070 --> 01:17:49,602
So low-dimensional embeddings have some bad properties,

1824
01:17:49,602 --> 01:17:51,810
and maybe it's just really hard to use dimensionality

1825
01:17:51,810 --> 01:17:55,530
of the embeddings as the way to learn a good representation.

1826
01:17:55,530 --> 01:18:00,730
Hypothesis two is autoencoders are learning shortcuts.

1827
01:18:00,730 --> 01:18:03,888
So in order for me to reconstruct the signal--

1828
01:18:03,888 --> 01:18:04,430
imagine this.

1829
01:18:04,430 --> 01:18:06,910
What if I had an autoencoder with skip connections,

1830
01:18:06,910 --> 01:18:08,210
with residual connections?

1831
01:18:08,210 --> 01:18:10,910
Would that be a good idea?

1832
01:18:10,910 --> 01:18:13,392
What would be wrong with that, autoencoder f,

1833
01:18:13,392 --> 01:18:15,350
g, but they just have some residual connections

1834
01:18:15,350 --> 01:18:17,513
in between f and g?

1835
01:18:17,513 --> 01:18:19,930
AUDIENCE: Not forcing this low-dimensional representation.

1836
01:18:19,930 --> 01:18:20,030
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1837
01:18:20,030 --> 01:18:21,453
It skips the bottleneck.

1838
01:18:21,453 --> 01:18:23,370
So there's all these little gotchas like that.

1839
01:18:23,370 --> 01:18:24,953
And it could be that autoencoders just

1840
01:18:24,953 --> 01:18:30,390
have this tendency to copy the local information that they're

1841
01:18:30,390 --> 01:18:31,130
processing.

1842
01:18:31,130 --> 01:18:33,170
And that will be a decent solution,

1843
01:18:33,170 --> 01:18:35,543
even though it would be better for them

1844
01:18:35,543 --> 01:18:37,710
to capture global properties in order to reconstruct

1845
01:18:37,710 --> 01:18:39,990
the entire signal.

1846
01:18:39,990 --> 01:18:41,435
And then maybe math prediction is

1847
01:18:41,435 --> 01:18:43,810
closer to the prediction problems we actually care about.

1848
01:18:43,810 --> 01:18:45,730
So somehow it's closer to the actual use case.

1849
01:18:45,730 --> 01:18:47,980
We're going to try to predict the future from the past

1850
01:18:47,980 --> 01:18:49,390
or something like this.

1851
01:18:49,390 --> 01:18:50,830
So I don't know.

1852
01:18:50,830 --> 01:18:56,462
I asked Kaiming, who's a professor here who was the first

1853
01:18:56,462 --> 01:18:58,170
author of "The Masked Autoencoder" paper.

1854
01:18:58,170 --> 01:19:00,890
And he said, well, at the end of the day, it's just empiricism.

1855
01:19:00,890 --> 01:19:04,410
So mass prediction works better than autoencoding.

1856
01:19:04,410 --> 01:19:07,160
And I think it's a really interesting question,

1857
01:19:07,160 --> 01:19:09,080
theoretically why that should be the case.

1858
01:19:09,080 --> 01:19:09,720
Yeah?

1859
01:19:09,720 --> 01:19:11,678
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] beginning of the lecture,

1860
01:19:11,678 --> 01:19:14,480
you said that in the scope of representation learning,

1861
01:19:14,480 --> 01:19:19,320
you think this framework of [INAUDIBLE] is simplest

1862
01:19:19,320 --> 01:19:20,225
and will win out.

1863
01:19:20,225 --> 01:19:21,600
I was wondering if you could talk

1864
01:19:21,600 --> 01:19:24,640
about why you think that is?

1865
01:19:24,640 --> 01:19:26,480
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so ultimately, it just

1866
01:19:26,480 --> 01:19:28,337
goes back to some first principle argument

1867
01:19:28,337 --> 01:19:29,420
that I can't really prove.

1868
01:19:29,420 --> 01:19:34,300
But there are arguments along the lines of Occam's razor

1869
01:19:34,300 --> 01:19:39,160
and formalisms of that the compression is somehow

1870
01:19:39,160 --> 01:19:40,977
equivalent to prediction.

1871
01:19:40,977 --> 01:19:42,560
And the most compressed representation

1872
01:19:42,560 --> 01:19:45,240
will make the most accurate predictions about the future.

1873
01:19:45,240 --> 01:19:47,760
And autoencoders are just a simple embodiment

1874
01:19:47,760 --> 01:19:51,360
of the idea of take your data, compress it as much as possible,

1875
01:19:51,360 --> 01:19:54,500
and remove all redundancies.

1876
01:19:54,500 --> 01:19:58,260
And it just seems, from first principles,

1877
01:19:58,260 --> 01:19:59,760
if compression is all you need, then

1878
01:19:59,760 --> 01:20:01,400
autoencoders are all you need.

1879
01:20:01,400 --> 01:20:03,025
I think there's more to say about that.

1880
01:20:03,025 --> 01:20:05,140
I don't have time right now.

